WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:05.800 --> 00:00:07.480
Hello, everyone.

00:00:07.480 --> 00:00:13.000
And welcome to
lecture 4 of CME 296.

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:17.320
So before we start, I just want
to say that those first three

00:00:17.320 --> 00:00:20.200
lectures were really
the most technical

00:00:20.200 --> 00:00:22.440
and mathematically challenging.

00:00:22.440 --> 00:00:25.720
So in that sense, I'm
still glad you're here.

00:00:25.720 --> 00:00:30.440
And I really hope that those
three lectures gave you

00:00:30.440 --> 00:00:34.440
the intuition as to why we were
going to use this generation

00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:35.520
paradigm.

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:38.040
Because there are going
to be used as building

00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:42.680
blocks for everything that
we're going to see from now on.

00:00:42.680 --> 00:00:47.120
In particular, during
the first three lectures,

00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:51.960
we focused on
unconditional generation.

00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:54.600
And now, what we're
going to see is

00:00:54.600 --> 00:00:58.640
how we can generate images
with a condition which

00:00:58.640 --> 00:01:05.950
can be a user prompt, like
input text, or an input image.

00:01:05.950 --> 00:01:09.390
And so in that sense, the
title of today's lecture

00:01:09.390 --> 00:01:13.870
is multi-modal
guided generation.

00:01:13.870 --> 00:01:17.310
With that, we're going to start.

00:01:17.310 --> 00:01:20.110
And as usual, I'm
going to just recap

00:01:20.110 --> 00:01:22.910
what we did the previous times.

00:01:22.910 --> 00:01:26.190
So as I mentioned, the
first three lectures

00:01:26.190 --> 00:01:28.390
were mathematically
challenging, and they

00:01:28.390 --> 00:01:31.750
were about seeing
how we could generate

00:01:31.750 --> 00:01:35.550
images, what were the
paradigms that we could use.

00:01:35.550 --> 00:01:39.830
So the first lecture was focused
on the diffusion paradigm

00:01:39.830 --> 00:01:44.750
with DDPM, where we saw that
we were taking clean images,

00:01:44.750 --> 00:01:48.950
that we were noising
in a discrete way.

00:01:48.950 --> 00:01:51.210
And so here, we
had this formula,

00:01:51.210 --> 00:01:56.190
which was a combination of a
clean image and Gaussian noise

00:01:56.190 --> 00:02:00.860
that we weighted with
those coefficients.

00:02:00.860 --> 00:02:03.380
And what we saw
with a bunch of math

00:02:03.380 --> 00:02:08.060
was that we could
derive an expression

00:02:08.060 --> 00:02:11.820
that we could optimize
in order to reconstruct

00:02:11.820 --> 00:02:15.380
those clean images
that we've noised.

00:02:15.380 --> 00:02:21.540
And we ended up with a very
nice expression, an L2 loss

00:02:21.540 --> 00:02:23.780
on the noise that was added.

00:02:23.780 --> 00:02:27.140
And in particular, if
we wanted to denoise,

00:02:27.140 --> 00:02:30.860
we had the following
formula that allowed you

00:02:30.860 --> 00:02:37.300
to go from a step t to step
t minus 1 using this model e

00:02:37.300 --> 00:02:41.380
theta, which was the predicted
noise that was added.

00:02:41.380 --> 00:02:45.020
And so in that sense, it was
parameterized with respect

00:02:45.020 --> 00:02:47.700
to the noise epsilon.

00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:50.660
Then in lecture
2, what we did was

00:02:50.660 --> 00:02:57.250
that we derived the continuous
version of what we derived

00:02:57.250 --> 00:02:59.650
in a discrete way in lecture 1.

00:02:59.650 --> 00:03:01.750
And we also saw a
second paradigm,

00:03:01.750 --> 00:03:06.250
a second way of seeing things,
which was score matching.

00:03:06.250 --> 00:03:10.010
And we saw that when
we looked at these two

00:03:10.010 --> 00:03:18.610
ways of doing things, if we were
to take the increment of time

00:03:18.610 --> 00:03:23.650
from t to t plus 1
and tend it to 0,

00:03:23.650 --> 00:03:28.090
we had a general
expression of the increment

00:03:28.090 --> 00:03:32.190
in x, which would be
as a function of dt,

00:03:32.190 --> 00:03:35.770
the increment in
time, and dw, which

00:03:35.770 --> 00:03:38.890
is the continuous
version of adding noise.

00:03:38.890 --> 00:03:41.230
And in particular, you
had these two terms.

00:03:41.230 --> 00:03:47.450
So on the left side, the drift
term, and then on the right side

00:03:47.450 --> 00:03:50.090
was the diffusion term.

00:03:50.090 --> 00:03:54.000
And we saw that in order
to reverse the noise that

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:57.200
was added, so a.k.a.
reverse process,

00:03:57.200 --> 00:04:01.440
we could use a
result from theories

00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:05.000
on stochastic
differential equations,

00:04:05.000 --> 00:04:11.160
where we saw that we could
derive the reverse process if we

00:04:11.160 --> 00:04:15.080
knew the score, which is
why this formulation was

00:04:15.080 --> 00:04:18.600
parameterized as a
function of the score.

00:04:18.600 --> 00:04:22.040
So here it's noted S of theta.

00:04:22.040 --> 00:04:26.200
And in lecture three,
we saw that actually, we

00:04:26.200 --> 00:04:29.240
could think of this whole
thing as a completely--

00:04:29.240 --> 00:04:31.200
in a completely different way.

00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:35.400
And we can think of it as
transporting a probability

00:04:35.400 --> 00:04:38.160
mass from an
initial distribution

00:04:38.160 --> 00:04:40.160
up to a target distribution.

00:04:40.160 --> 00:04:45.680
And in that sense, we
could express a noisy image

00:04:45.680 --> 00:04:50.160
as a combination
of noise and sample

00:04:50.160 --> 00:04:52.230
from the target distribution.

00:04:52.230 --> 00:04:56.470
And all of what we had
to do was to just predict

00:04:56.470 --> 00:04:58.630
the vector field, a.k.a.

00:04:58.630 --> 00:05:04.110
The velocity, which
was noted u of theta.

00:05:04.110 --> 00:05:09.790
But one thing that
we kept silent

00:05:09.790 --> 00:05:13.870
was how we could
actually represent

00:05:13.870 --> 00:05:17.830
all of these quantities,
in particular xt,

00:05:17.830 --> 00:05:22.750
the representation of the image
when it's noised at a given

00:05:22.750 --> 00:05:28.510
level, the noise itself.

00:05:28.510 --> 00:05:31.110
All of these, we just
assumed that there

00:05:31.110 --> 00:05:35.230
was some way of
representing these in some n

00:05:35.230 --> 00:05:37.790
or d dimensional space.

00:05:37.790 --> 00:05:39.830
So one thing that
we will do today

00:05:39.830 --> 00:05:43.470
is to see what
space could actually

00:05:43.470 --> 00:05:48.430
be more appropriate
for this kind of work.

00:05:48.430 --> 00:05:52.140
And then the second
thing is, as you can see,

00:05:52.140 --> 00:05:58.260
the models that we need to
estimate-- so epsilon theta, s

00:05:58.260 --> 00:05:59.560
of theta, you have theta.

00:05:59.560 --> 00:06:05.900
There are only a function of
denoised image and the time,

00:06:05.900 --> 00:06:11.500
but they're not a function of
any condition, any user input.

00:06:11.500 --> 00:06:15.300
So this is also something that
we're going to see, a.k.a.

00:06:15.300 --> 00:06:18.180
How can we incorporate
a condition

00:06:18.180 --> 00:06:24.220
that could guide the generation
towards what we want.

00:06:24.220 --> 00:06:26.740
So this is the menu for today.

00:06:26.740 --> 00:06:30.900
So to recap, we're going
to see three things.

00:06:30.900 --> 00:06:36.140
The first one is how can we
represent those noisy images.

00:06:36.140 --> 00:06:37.860
The second thing that
we're going to see

00:06:37.860 --> 00:06:40.860
is how to represent
the conditions

00:06:40.860 --> 00:06:42.840
that we want as inputs.

00:06:42.840 --> 00:06:45.880
So for instance,
the input text here,

00:06:45.880 --> 00:06:49.690
a Teddy bear reading a book that
we want to generate an image for

00:06:49.690 --> 00:06:52.850
or an input image that
you want to condition

00:06:52.850 --> 00:06:54.970
your generation for.

00:06:54.970 --> 00:06:59.170
And then the third thing is
how we can guide the generation

00:06:59.170 --> 00:07:04.850
process using that as an input.

00:07:04.850 --> 00:07:07.550
So we're going to
start with number one,

00:07:07.550 --> 00:07:12.730
how to represent
these noisy images.

00:07:12.730 --> 00:07:17.170
And I'm going to start with
the most natural approach

00:07:17.170 --> 00:07:21.410
that you could have when
you see those images.

00:07:21.410 --> 00:07:26.170
We, as humans, we are very
accustomed to seeing things

00:07:26.170 --> 00:07:28.210
in the pixel space.

00:07:28.210 --> 00:07:31.330
So here, if you take
this image as an example,

00:07:31.330 --> 00:07:37.530
this image has a certain
height H, certain width, W.

00:07:37.530 --> 00:07:42.810
So what you can do is
to represent each pixel

00:07:42.810 --> 00:07:49.320
as a function of the intensity
of the color that composes it.

00:07:49.320 --> 00:07:53.200
And in particular, a very
common representation

00:07:53.200 --> 00:07:58.680
is using R, so red,
green, and blue.

00:07:58.680 --> 00:08:02.080
So in other words,
you would have

00:08:02.080 --> 00:08:05.280
each pixel that would
have three values, which

00:08:05.280 --> 00:08:07.360
would make up the color.

00:08:07.360 --> 00:08:12.600
And you would have these values
cover all pixels of the image.

00:08:12.600 --> 00:08:15.280
So you would have
a dimension n that

00:08:15.280 --> 00:08:20.180
would be equal to 3 times the
size, the number of pixels,

00:08:20.180 --> 00:08:24.800
which is h times w.

00:08:24.800 --> 00:08:27.760
And so you could
represent your image

00:08:27.760 --> 00:08:33.640
as a tensor along the axis,
the height, the width,

00:08:33.640 --> 00:08:37.120
and the number of
channels here, which

00:08:37.120 --> 00:08:40.760
are three, which
represents R, G, and B.

00:08:40.760 --> 00:08:42.640
So this could be one way.

00:08:42.640 --> 00:08:49.630
But the big problem with that is
that it's very high dimension.

00:08:49.630 --> 00:08:54.570
So if we take the example of a,
quote, unquote, "regular image,"

00:08:54.570 --> 00:09:01.270
so 1,024 times 1,024 pixels,
then we would end up with

00:09:01.270 --> 00:09:04.870
a number of dimensions,
which would be on the order

00:09:04.870 --> 00:09:06.270
of a million.

00:09:06.270 --> 00:09:09.190
So 10 to the power of 6.

00:09:09.190 --> 00:09:12.510
And if you think
about it, this is just

00:09:12.510 --> 00:09:17.150
a lot of dimensions to consider
in our training generation

00:09:17.150 --> 00:09:20.290
paradigm algorithms,
which are, of course,

00:09:20.290 --> 00:09:24.830
a function of the dimension
in which you operate.

00:09:24.830 --> 00:09:28.870
So that's one very big drawback.

00:09:28.870 --> 00:09:32.830
The second drawback is if
you look at your image,

00:09:32.830 --> 00:09:37.190
you actually see that you
have a lot of redundancies.

00:09:37.190 --> 00:09:41.470
So if you see, for instance, in
a given neighborhood of pixels,

00:09:41.470 --> 00:09:44.420
you have a lot of
very similar values.

00:09:44.420 --> 00:09:51.120
So in a sense, you can consider
this as a waste of dimensions.

00:09:51.120 --> 00:09:54.340
So it's not very compact.

00:09:54.340 --> 00:09:57.140
And then the third
thing is if you

00:09:57.140 --> 00:10:00.660
remember our whole
first three lectures

00:10:00.660 --> 00:10:03.740
were about taking the
image, and adding noise,

00:10:03.740 --> 00:10:07.220
and then just moving it
in the space with respect

00:10:07.220 --> 00:10:09.660
to added noise.

00:10:09.660 --> 00:10:12.580
Well, here, if
you take an image,

00:10:12.580 --> 00:10:15.100
and you were to move it
slightly in that space

00:10:15.100 --> 00:10:17.700
by adding some noise,
you would actually

00:10:17.700 --> 00:10:21.420
obtain an image, a noisy
image that would actually not

00:10:21.420 --> 00:10:24.340
be very meaningful.

00:10:24.340 --> 00:10:27.860
It would not mean anything.

00:10:27.860 --> 00:10:29.860
So in particular, so
I'm not sure if you

00:10:29.860 --> 00:10:34.860
recall this nice
graphs or charts, where

00:10:34.860 --> 00:10:40.580
you would see the areas of
high density of your target

00:10:40.580 --> 00:10:42.610
desired distribution.

00:10:42.610 --> 00:10:49.450
So here, what we want is
clusters of observations

00:10:49.450 --> 00:10:51.250
that are likely to occur.

00:10:51.250 --> 00:10:53.530
But what we get is
actually the thing

00:10:53.530 --> 00:10:58.930
on the right, which is a space
with very spiky regions, where

00:10:58.930 --> 00:11:04.370
each spike represents
an actual valid image.

00:11:04.370 --> 00:11:07.490
And that's not good.

00:11:07.490 --> 00:11:10.530
So for all these
reasons, we're going

00:11:10.530 --> 00:11:15.330
to just come up with
a wish list of a space

00:11:15.330 --> 00:11:17.410
that we would like to have.

00:11:17.410 --> 00:11:21.010
So I'm going to go
through that step by step.

00:11:21.010 --> 00:11:24.610
So the first one is the high
dimensionality observation

00:11:24.610 --> 00:11:25.770
that I made.

00:11:25.770 --> 00:11:29.290
So here, what we want is a
dimension that's more, quote,

00:11:29.290 --> 00:11:31.410
unquote, "tractable."

00:11:31.410 --> 00:11:33.370
So that's number one.

00:11:33.370 --> 00:11:37.210
Number two of our wish list is
to have a representation that

00:11:37.210 --> 00:11:40.480
does not have a lot of
redundancies, that has

00:11:40.480 --> 00:11:44.520
some way of compacting things.

00:11:44.520 --> 00:11:49.800
So number 2 is compact
representation.

00:11:49.800 --> 00:11:52.560
Number 3, regarding
the representation

00:11:52.560 --> 00:11:54.520
not being meaningful,
so here, we

00:11:54.520 --> 00:11:58.040
want a representation
that is meaningful.

00:11:58.040 --> 00:12:04.160
And you can think of this when
I say what meaningful means.

00:12:04.160 --> 00:12:06.800
It's when you look at
the space, and you're

00:12:06.800 --> 00:12:14.800
able to have clusters of regions
where valid images happen.

00:12:14.800 --> 00:12:19.120
That's what I mean
by meaningful space.

00:12:19.120 --> 00:12:22.240
So this is our wish list.

00:12:22.240 --> 00:12:27.280
So before we move ahead,
I want to take the time

00:12:27.280 --> 00:12:31.800
to introduce two
terminologies that are widely

00:12:31.800 --> 00:12:33.680
used in those papers.

00:12:33.680 --> 00:12:36.760
So I prefer to define
them now and clearly

00:12:36.760 --> 00:12:40.070
so that you what they refer to.

00:12:40.070 --> 00:12:42.070
So you will see
that there is a lot

00:12:42.070 --> 00:12:46.170
talk about semantic similarity.

00:12:46.170 --> 00:12:49.110
So what does semantic
similarity mean?

00:12:49.110 --> 00:12:52.950
So semantic
similarity of an image

00:12:52.950 --> 00:12:57.230
is something that refers
to its global geometry

00:12:57.230 --> 00:12:59.590
to its overall structure.

00:12:59.590 --> 00:13:01.910
And in particular,
what that means

00:13:01.910 --> 00:13:07.710
is if two images are
semantically similar,

00:13:07.710 --> 00:13:10.510
what that means is
that they roughly

00:13:10.510 --> 00:13:13.310
represent the same thing.

00:13:13.310 --> 00:13:15.350
So here on the slide,
you have on the left

00:13:15.350 --> 00:13:17.710
a Teddy bear that
is reading a book.

00:13:17.710 --> 00:13:19.510
On the right, you
have also a Teddy bear

00:13:19.510 --> 00:13:20.610
that is reading a book.

00:13:20.610 --> 00:13:24.310
They're not exactly
aligned pixel wise,

00:13:24.310 --> 00:13:27.950
but they're
semantically similar.

00:13:27.950 --> 00:13:31.070
So that's for
semantic similarity.

00:13:31.070 --> 00:13:38.340
Now, you have another notation,
which is perceptual similarity.

00:13:38.340 --> 00:13:45.940
So this one refers to more of
the local details of an image.

00:13:45.940 --> 00:13:49.540
More to the lower level
texture of an image.

00:13:49.540 --> 00:13:55.100
And when you say that two images
are perceptually similar, what

00:13:55.100 --> 00:14:00.780
that means is that to the
human eye, they look the same.

00:14:00.780 --> 00:14:02.800
So for instance,
you have, again,

00:14:02.800 --> 00:14:04.640
two pictures of a Teddy bear.

00:14:04.640 --> 00:14:06.940
So I'm not sure if you
see the one on the right

00:14:06.940 --> 00:14:09.220
has some little glasses.

00:14:09.220 --> 00:14:12.080
But it's perceptually
very similar.

00:14:15.220 --> 00:14:19.020
Does that make sense,
that distinction?

00:14:19.020 --> 00:14:19.900
Yeah.

00:14:19.900 --> 00:14:21.700
The reason why I say
that is I'm going

00:14:21.700 --> 00:14:23.560
to use that terminology
going forward,

00:14:23.560 --> 00:14:28.420
so I just want to make
sure we all what these are.

00:14:28.420 --> 00:14:31.340
So what are we doing?

00:14:31.340 --> 00:14:35.690
What we want is to come
up with a space that

00:14:35.690 --> 00:14:41.690
fulfills our wish list
that we established before.

00:14:41.690 --> 00:14:44.370
So here is our first attempt.

00:14:44.370 --> 00:14:50.690
So how about we take an
image in the pixel space,

00:14:50.690 --> 00:14:56.250
and what we try to do
is find a representation

00:14:56.250 --> 00:15:02.130
that's meaningful using
the following architecture.

00:15:02.130 --> 00:15:05.130
So let's assume we
take that image,

00:15:05.130 --> 00:15:08.170
and we put it into some model.

00:15:08.170 --> 00:15:13.290
Let's call it an encoder
that encodes the image

00:15:13.290 --> 00:15:17.650
and produces an output
that is of lower

00:15:17.650 --> 00:15:20.650
dimensionality as the input.

00:15:20.650 --> 00:15:26.290
So here, we go from an
image of size HW and number

00:15:26.290 --> 00:15:28.690
of channels, which,
as you know, is

00:15:28.690 --> 00:15:32.320
equal to 3 in the pixel
space because in the RGB

00:15:32.320 --> 00:15:34.720
representation.

00:15:34.720 --> 00:15:37.920
And here, we end up with
a latent representation

00:15:37.920 --> 00:15:45.880
of size lowercase h,
lowercase w, and little c.

00:15:45.880 --> 00:15:51.520
And then what we do is we
put that representation back

00:15:51.520 --> 00:15:52.380
to the decoder.

00:15:52.380 --> 00:15:57.080
So if we were to do
that, our objective

00:15:57.080 --> 00:16:02.800
is to recover the
original image.

00:16:02.800 --> 00:16:05.240
So we don't want to
lose any information

00:16:05.240 --> 00:16:09.060
by encoding our image into
a lower dimensional space.

00:16:09.060 --> 00:16:16.640
So here, such a setup is
called an autoencoder.

00:16:16.640 --> 00:16:19.880
So the only job
of an autoencoder

00:16:19.880 --> 00:16:26.240
is to find a lower dimension
representation that's called

00:16:26.240 --> 00:16:29.240
the latent representation.

00:16:29.240 --> 00:16:32.950
And what it does
us is take an image

00:16:32.950 --> 00:16:40.190
in the pixel space as input,
compress it, and then decode it.

00:16:40.190 --> 00:16:45.390
And here, the task is making
sure that what you get as output

00:16:45.390 --> 00:16:47.490
is the same as what
you get as input.

00:16:50.270 --> 00:16:55.270
So you may think, OK, it's
a very useless model, which

00:16:55.270 --> 00:16:59.710
I agree, unless you're
interested in the latent

00:16:59.710 --> 00:17:02.350
space, which we are.

00:17:02.350 --> 00:17:03.810
This is what we're
interested in.

00:17:06.430 --> 00:17:08.050
So does this setup make sense?

00:17:11.510 --> 00:17:12.010
Cool.

00:17:12.010 --> 00:17:16.829
And then terminology wise, the
latent space, like that part,

00:17:16.829 --> 00:17:20.190
is called the bottleneck just
because we're forcing the model

00:17:20.190 --> 00:17:22.990
to rely on fewer
dimensions, and we

00:17:22.990 --> 00:17:29.420
want the model to still preserve
the richness of the details that

00:17:29.420 --> 00:17:32.860
are present in the image.

00:17:32.860 --> 00:17:34.760
So let's see how we do that.

00:17:34.760 --> 00:17:38.580
But before we go, I want to
introduce another notation

00:17:38.580 --> 00:17:40.660
that you also see a
lot in papers, which

00:17:40.660 --> 00:17:46.140
is called the spatial
compression ratio, which

00:17:46.140 --> 00:17:52.300
is defined by the amount
by which you go from Big H

00:17:52.300 --> 00:17:54.220
to little h.

00:17:54.220 --> 00:17:57.860
So it's just the
ratio between the two.

00:17:57.860 --> 00:18:02.980
And that ratio is typically
on the order of 8.

00:18:02.980 --> 00:18:08.420
So that's the order of magnitude
that you should have in mind.

00:18:08.420 --> 00:18:08.960
Cool.

00:18:08.960 --> 00:18:12.620
So let's see now based
on what I told you

00:18:12.620 --> 00:18:17.420
how each component works.

00:18:17.420 --> 00:18:23.120
So in terms of what is input in
the encoder, as we mentioned,

00:18:23.120 --> 00:18:26.000
we have our input image.

00:18:26.000 --> 00:18:33.530
Let's note it x that we put in
our encoder, that we note E phi.

00:18:33.530 --> 00:18:36.490
And we get a latent
representation z

00:18:36.490 --> 00:18:40.810
that is equal to E phi of x.

00:18:40.810 --> 00:18:43.770
And so the way we
would do that would

00:18:43.770 --> 00:18:49.130
be to use a network that
contains some downsampling

00:18:49.130 --> 00:18:50.690
operations.

00:18:50.690 --> 00:18:54.570
So what that means is it
allows us to go from a bigger

00:18:54.570 --> 00:18:58.050
dimension to a lower dimension.

00:18:58.050 --> 00:19:01.090
We're not going to go
into too much details

00:19:01.090 --> 00:19:04.770
into what that network is
because we're going to talk more

00:19:04.770 --> 00:19:07.250
about this in the next lecture.

00:19:07.250 --> 00:19:11.570
But I just want to tell you
roughly the two main operations

00:19:11.570 --> 00:19:15.110
that you can expect,
which you may have seen.

00:19:15.110 --> 00:19:18.090
So the first one
is convolutions.

00:19:18.090 --> 00:19:20.810
So who has heard of
convolutions before?

00:19:20.810 --> 00:19:21.750
Yeah, everyone.

00:19:21.750 --> 00:19:23.690
So I can go fast.

00:19:23.690 --> 00:19:28.760
Convolutions is just a way to
scan your image using a filter.

00:19:28.760 --> 00:19:31.300
So the image is in red,
the filter is in blue.

00:19:31.300 --> 00:19:36.040
And what you get is what we call
an activation map or a feature

00:19:36.040 --> 00:19:41.120
map that is in purple.

00:19:41.120 --> 00:19:42.720
And then the second
operation that you

00:19:42.720 --> 00:19:46.480
see in these kinds of
networks is pooling.

00:19:46.480 --> 00:19:50.440
So pooling is a
downsampling operator

00:19:50.440 --> 00:19:55.040
that allows you to either take
the maximum of a given square,

00:19:55.040 --> 00:19:59.640
or the average, or either max
pooling or average pooling.

00:19:59.640 --> 00:20:03.400
And it allows you to introduce
some spatial invariance

00:20:03.400 --> 00:20:08.960
just because you can aggregate
the pixels to a higher

00:20:08.960 --> 00:20:11.268
granularity.

00:20:11.268 --> 00:20:13.060
We're not going to go
into too much detail,

00:20:13.060 --> 00:20:16.440
but these are just
for reference.

00:20:16.440 --> 00:20:19.480
So when it comes to the
decoder, what you would do

00:20:19.480 --> 00:20:22.480
is take the latent
representation

00:20:22.480 --> 00:20:27.070
z into the decoder d theta.

00:20:27.070 --> 00:20:32.190
And you would get your
reconstructed output x hat.

00:20:32.190 --> 00:20:36.030
So here, you would use
upsampling operations,

00:20:36.030 --> 00:20:40.310
for instance reverse
convolutions.

00:20:40.310 --> 00:20:45.590
And the goal of this model
is as much as possible,

00:20:45.590 --> 00:20:49.990
for your reconstructed
output to be as close as

00:20:49.990 --> 00:20:53.030
possible to your input.

00:20:53.030 --> 00:20:54.990
So the loss function
of such a model

00:20:54.990 --> 00:20:59.910
would be something along the
lines of just an L2 regression

00:20:59.910 --> 00:21:06.130
between the input x and the
reconstructed input x hat.

00:21:08.750 --> 00:21:10.710
So I have a question for you.

00:21:10.710 --> 00:21:13.250
Do you think this
model is a good model?

00:21:18.350 --> 00:21:20.630
So this is what we're
going to see now.

00:21:20.630 --> 00:21:22.700
So let's go through
our checklist.

00:21:22.700 --> 00:21:27.380
First one is, OK., is the
dimension more tractable?

00:21:27.380 --> 00:21:29.580
Yes, more tractable.

00:21:29.580 --> 00:21:32.540
Is the representation
more compact?

00:21:32.540 --> 00:21:35.380
Yes, it's more compact.

00:21:35.380 --> 00:21:38.400
Is the representation
meaningful?

00:21:38.400 --> 00:21:42.980
Meaning do you have a nice
shape in your latent space

00:21:42.980 --> 00:21:46.380
in the way we represent it?

00:21:46.380 --> 00:21:47.320
Yes, exactly.

00:21:47.320 --> 00:21:49.100
Not necessarily.

00:21:49.100 --> 00:21:52.460
Well, the reason for
that is we have not

00:21:52.460 --> 00:21:55.340
constrained our latent
space to be structured

00:21:55.340 --> 00:21:57.460
in any particular way.

00:21:57.460 --> 00:22:01.020
So the model is just
only incentivized

00:22:01.020 --> 00:22:06.460
to reconstruct the
input as best as it can.

00:22:06.460 --> 00:22:12.260
And it really doesn't care
what shape the latent space is.

00:22:12.260 --> 00:22:14.780
So you actually still
end up with this kind

00:22:14.780 --> 00:22:17.900
of space, which is not good.

00:22:17.900 --> 00:22:21.550
This is not good because
your flow matching model,

00:22:21.550 --> 00:22:25.930
your diffusion model is just
not able to learn very well

00:22:25.930 --> 00:22:28.810
in those spaces
because, first of all,

00:22:28.810 --> 00:22:33.130
the representations
are all over the place.

00:22:33.130 --> 00:22:36.490
And if you remember,
one common thing

00:22:36.490 --> 00:22:39.410
with all of these
generation paradigms

00:22:39.410 --> 00:22:43.490
is that you start from
a normal distribution,

00:22:43.490 --> 00:22:45.970
like a standard
normal distribution.

00:22:45.970 --> 00:22:49.770
So you need a way for
you to not get lost.

00:22:49.770 --> 00:22:54.970
So if everything is far
apart, it just doesn't work.

00:22:54.970 --> 00:22:57.010
So that's not good.

00:22:57.010 --> 00:22:59.650
So let's go back to
the drawing board,

00:22:59.650 --> 00:23:02.850
and let's see what
we can change.

00:23:02.850 --> 00:23:06.370
So we take our image,
we compress it,

00:23:06.370 --> 00:23:08.370
and then we decompress it.

00:23:08.370 --> 00:23:10.090
Everything's perfect.

00:23:10.090 --> 00:23:15.650
Well, let's see what we
can do in the latent space.

00:23:15.650 --> 00:23:21.120
Our goal here would be to,
quote, unquote, "enforce"

00:23:21.120 --> 00:23:25.040
the latent space to
have some structure.

00:23:25.040 --> 00:23:27.520
So this is our goal.

00:23:27.520 --> 00:23:29.900
And we're going to see
how we can do that.

00:23:32.480 --> 00:23:35.140
OK, let's go back
to where we were.

00:23:35.140 --> 00:23:39.240
We take our input image x.

00:23:39.240 --> 00:23:46.280
But instead of just letting it
through a standard encoder, what

00:23:46.280 --> 00:23:53.480
we will do is to actually
not try to predict

00:23:53.480 --> 00:23:59.320
or generate the latent
representation directly.

00:23:59.320 --> 00:24:07.320
We will actually predict
two things, the mean

00:24:07.320 --> 00:24:11.120
and the standard deviation
of the distribution

00:24:11.120 --> 00:24:14.960
that it can be drawn from.

00:24:14.960 --> 00:24:20.110
So in other words, what we're
going to see to say is instead

00:24:20.110 --> 00:24:26.270
of saying that one x corresponds
to one latent representation,

00:24:26.270 --> 00:24:32.990
we're going to say that 1x
actually is mapped to some

00:24:32.990 --> 00:24:39.190
probability distribution, which
sounds kind of weird, right?

00:24:39.190 --> 00:24:41.830
It's not very natural.

00:24:41.830 --> 00:24:45.870
But we're going to see why
that mitigates the problem.

00:24:45.870 --> 00:24:48.790
So here, as a reminder, so
the output of the encoder

00:24:48.790 --> 00:24:53.430
will not be just
a latent vector.

00:24:53.430 --> 00:24:58.410
It will be-- so the mean mu phi.

00:24:58.410 --> 00:25:03.070
And the variance
sigma squared phi.

00:25:03.070 --> 00:25:06.910
So these are the two
outputs of the encoder.

00:25:06.910 --> 00:25:10.750
And what we will say is that
the latent representation

00:25:10.750 --> 00:25:16.732
of this distribution,
this latent representation

00:25:16.732 --> 00:25:18.440
is actually drawn from
this distribution.

00:25:18.440 --> 00:25:21.860
That's what we're going to say.

00:25:21.860 --> 00:25:25.180
And we're going to
say something else.

00:25:25.180 --> 00:25:28.580
So in a bit of an
informal way, we're

00:25:28.580 --> 00:25:34.100
going to say that this
distribution is going

00:25:34.100 --> 00:25:39.260
to be roughly forced to
follow or to approximate

00:25:39.260 --> 00:25:42.380
another distribution.

00:25:42.380 --> 00:25:44.400
So it's not very
mathematically rigorous.

00:25:44.400 --> 00:25:48.220
We were going to see exactly
how we can express that.

00:25:48.220 --> 00:25:49.820
But in your mind,
I want you to know

00:25:49.820 --> 00:25:55.700
that this is a condition for
us to structure the latent

00:25:55.700 --> 00:25:59.500
space in a way where
we say the latent

00:25:59.500 --> 00:26:03.740
representation of an
input x will be roughly

00:26:03.740 --> 00:26:05.480
in a given region of the space.

00:26:05.480 --> 00:26:07.100
We're going to
force that, or we're

00:26:07.100 --> 00:26:11.820
going to incentivize
the model to learn that.

00:26:11.820 --> 00:26:12.920
So that's the idea.

00:26:17.380 --> 00:26:17.980
Cool.

00:26:17.980 --> 00:26:21.340
And yeah, this distribution is--

00:26:21.340 --> 00:26:22.900
OK, you may have guessed it.

00:26:22.900 --> 00:26:26.660
Again, standard normal
distribution just because it's.

00:26:26.660 --> 00:26:29.963
It's a nice distribution
with great properties.

00:26:29.963 --> 00:26:31.380
And by the way,
this distribution,

00:26:31.380 --> 00:26:36.860
we're going to call it
the prior distribution.

00:26:36.860 --> 00:26:40.420
So let's see how
the decoder works.

00:26:40.420 --> 00:26:47.120
So let's assume we have put our
input image into the encoder.

00:26:47.120 --> 00:26:48.640
We have predicted the mean.

00:26:48.640 --> 00:26:50.960
We have predicted the
standard deviation.

00:26:50.960 --> 00:26:54.420
We have drawn a
latent representation

00:26:54.420 --> 00:26:57.380
from the distribution.

00:26:57.380 --> 00:26:59.020
So now, the decoding
stage will be

00:26:59.020 --> 00:27:02.380
around taking that
representation

00:27:02.380 --> 00:27:07.780
and passing it through the
decoder which again will output

00:27:07.780 --> 00:27:14.570
two things, the mean new theta
of this latent representation

00:27:14.570 --> 00:27:18.490
z and standard deviation.

00:27:18.490 --> 00:27:21.010
And what we're going
to say is, again,

00:27:21.010 --> 00:27:25.450
that the reconstructed
image is going

00:27:25.450 --> 00:27:30.530
to be drawn from a probability
distribution of these two

00:27:30.530 --> 00:27:31.390
parameters.

00:27:34.530 --> 00:27:38.850
Well, one thing that I
want to say is actually,

00:27:38.850 --> 00:27:41.050
we're going to
simplify this part

00:27:41.050 --> 00:27:42.770
of the problem a little bit.

00:27:42.770 --> 00:27:46.810
We're actually going to say that
the variance is not something

00:27:46.810 --> 00:27:47.950
we're going to predict.

00:27:47.950 --> 00:27:53.850
We're just going to fix it to
be constant just because there

00:27:53.850 --> 00:27:59.210
is less impact in you
making sure to introduce

00:27:59.210 --> 00:28:01.390
some variation in the output.

00:28:01.390 --> 00:28:04.130
Because in the pixel
space, even if you

00:28:04.130 --> 00:28:07.130
add a little bit
of noise, it's not

00:28:07.130 --> 00:28:10.840
going to change the
image a whole lot.

00:28:10.840 --> 00:28:13.800
So that's why people
just say, OK, we're

00:28:13.800 --> 00:28:17.620
going to take some variants
that's constant fixed,

00:28:17.620 --> 00:28:19.560
and we're not going
to predict that.

00:28:19.560 --> 00:28:21.600
We're only going to
predict the mean.

00:28:21.600 --> 00:28:24.200
We can draw from
this distribution.

00:28:24.200 --> 00:28:30.000
But sometimes, people also just
take the mean as the output.

00:28:30.000 --> 00:28:32.080
So this part is
more of a detail.

00:28:32.080 --> 00:28:32.760
Yeah.

00:28:32.760 --> 00:28:37.160
So the question here is
that whether the latency

00:28:37.160 --> 00:28:41.680
is drawn from the Gaussian
or is drawn from the q--

00:28:41.680 --> 00:28:45.740
the encoder here
q phi z given x.

00:28:45.740 --> 00:28:47.120
So it's a great question.

00:28:47.120 --> 00:28:53.040
So this model, which we're
going to see what the name is,

00:28:53.040 --> 00:28:57.480
is both a model that we can
use to learn the latent space,

00:28:57.480 --> 00:29:00.442
And so have a
reconstruction task.

00:29:00.442 --> 00:29:02.400
But it's also something
that can be interpreted

00:29:02.400 --> 00:29:04.520
as a generative model.

00:29:04.520 --> 00:29:08.830
So if your goal is to
reconstruct the input,

00:29:08.830 --> 00:29:13.990
then your input here is
going to be the z that you

00:29:13.990 --> 00:29:16.330
drew from that distribution.

00:29:16.330 --> 00:29:20.550
So it's going to be the
latent representation of x.

00:29:20.550 --> 00:29:26.990
But if your goal is to
generate just a new image,

00:29:26.990 --> 00:29:29.150
then you would
actually draw that z

00:29:29.150 --> 00:29:30.890
from the prior distribution.

00:29:30.890 --> 00:29:34.410
So the question is the encoder
gives you a mean and a variance.

00:29:34.410 --> 00:29:37.070
It does not give you something
deterministic exactly.

00:29:37.070 --> 00:29:40.430
It gives you a mean
and a variance.

00:29:40.430 --> 00:29:43.010
And what you're doing is
just drawing from that.

00:29:43.010 --> 00:29:45.990
So you-- yes, you
draw from that.

00:29:45.990 --> 00:29:47.290
So great question.

00:29:47.290 --> 00:29:50.870
So the question is, well, if you
have a generative model here,

00:29:50.870 --> 00:29:52.130
why would you bother.

00:29:52.130 --> 00:29:55.790
And just use diffusion, use flow
matching, use all these things?

00:29:55.790 --> 00:29:57.130
It's a great question.

00:29:57.130 --> 00:30:00.070
You have a number of
differences between the two

00:30:00.070 --> 00:30:03.710
which makes diffusion flow
matching and score actually

00:30:03.710 --> 00:30:05.620
perform better than VAE.

00:30:05.620 --> 00:30:12.020
One of the differences is
here, you only go from latent

00:30:12.020 --> 00:30:15.540
to pixel in just one step,
whereas the other ones,

00:30:15.540 --> 00:30:18.020
you have several steps
that just allows you

00:30:18.020 --> 00:30:20.440
to put more compute in there.

00:30:20.440 --> 00:30:21.600
That's one reason.

00:30:21.600 --> 00:30:23.460
But there are some
others that I hope we

00:30:23.460 --> 00:30:25.800
can get to if we get some time.

00:30:25.800 --> 00:30:29.240
But we're going to see the
relationship between the two.

00:30:29.240 --> 00:30:30.080
Another question?

00:30:30.080 --> 00:30:30.740
Yeah.

00:30:30.740 --> 00:30:33.380
The question is,
does the latent space

00:30:33.380 --> 00:30:35.340
look like a normal distribution?

00:30:35.340 --> 00:30:45.418
So there is something that makes
it be "regularized," quote,

00:30:45.418 --> 00:30:46.960
unquote, to be a
normal distribution.

00:30:46.960 --> 00:30:49.540
It depends on a
term that you can

00:30:49.540 --> 00:30:52.180
tweak with respect
to a coefficient

00:30:52.180 --> 00:30:53.620
that we're going to see.

00:30:53.620 --> 00:30:57.020
And depending on the
value of that coefficient,

00:30:57.020 --> 00:31:01.980
it may look more or less
than a normal distribution.

00:31:01.980 --> 00:31:03.280
But we're going to see that.

00:31:06.970 --> 00:31:07.510
Cool.

00:31:07.510 --> 00:31:10.270
So the first time I saw
this model, I was confused.

00:31:10.270 --> 00:31:13.090
You may be confused
now, and I hope

00:31:13.090 --> 00:31:18.170
what we're going to see will
make you a bit less confused.

00:31:18.170 --> 00:31:21.130
So here, one thing
that I want to say

00:31:21.130 --> 00:31:29.930
is in the autoencoder case, the
loss function was pretty clear.

00:31:29.930 --> 00:31:38.570
You would just try to estimate
the reconstructed output

00:31:38.570 --> 00:31:45.410
in such a way that it's as
close as possible to your input.

00:31:45.410 --> 00:31:48.970
But here, you have a bunch
of distributions everywhere.

00:31:48.970 --> 00:31:51.690
There's not like an obvious
way of expressing that

00:31:51.690 --> 00:31:54.010
without doing some math.

00:31:54.010 --> 00:31:59.410
So what I propose is that
we derive the loss function

00:31:59.410 --> 00:32:03.778
for this model, which I'm not
sure if I can tell it now,

00:32:03.778 --> 00:32:04.820
but I'm going to tell it.

00:32:04.820 --> 00:32:08.280
It's called the
variational autoencoder.

00:32:08.280 --> 00:32:12.920
And we're going to see
what makes it variational.

00:32:12.920 --> 00:32:20.680
So if you remember,
in a lot of cases,

00:32:20.680 --> 00:32:23.680
the way you derive
a loss function

00:32:23.680 --> 00:32:28.080
is by doing maximum
likelihood estimation.

00:32:28.080 --> 00:32:29.520
So what does that mean?

00:32:29.520 --> 00:32:34.600
That means that you have some
model of parameters theta.

00:32:34.600 --> 00:32:40.520
And what you want is to find
the parameters theta that

00:32:40.520 --> 00:32:48.000
maximize the probability of
your model seeing the data.

00:32:48.000 --> 00:32:49.680
That's what ML is.

00:32:49.680 --> 00:32:52.280
And that's what
we're going to use

00:32:52.280 --> 00:32:55.680
to see how we can derive
a loss function that

00:32:55.680 --> 00:32:58.960
can help us learn theta.

00:32:58.960 --> 00:33:03.950
So in particular, what
we want is to maximize

00:33:03.950 --> 00:33:06.310
the log of p theta of x.

00:33:06.310 --> 00:33:07.910
So I'm going to write it down.

00:33:07.910 --> 00:33:15.550
So what we want is to express
log p theta of x with respect

00:33:15.550 --> 00:33:20.430
to something that
we can compute.

00:33:20.430 --> 00:33:24.470
Because this, we cannot
really compute something just

00:33:24.470 --> 00:33:27.430
by looking at it.

00:33:27.430 --> 00:33:29.310
So let's see how we can do that.

00:33:29.310 --> 00:33:34.070
So I'm going to just start
with expressing p theta of x

00:33:34.070 --> 00:33:37.190
as a function of something.

00:33:37.190 --> 00:33:44.350
So this model can be seen
as a generative model that

00:33:44.350 --> 00:33:50.190
starts from a latent variable
that you draw from what

00:33:50.190 --> 00:33:53.710
we call a prior distribution.

00:33:53.710 --> 00:33:58.070
So one idea of
expressing p theta

00:33:58.070 --> 00:34:04.700
of x, if you remember
lecture 1, we can consider

00:34:04.700 --> 00:34:09.219
the joint probability
distribution of p theta

00:34:09.219 --> 00:34:21.580
of z, a latent, and x, and
sum across all latent z

00:34:21.580 --> 00:34:24.699
that led to x.

00:34:24.699 --> 00:34:27.340
We can do that, right?

00:34:27.340 --> 00:34:30.139
So what we say is
that p theta of x

00:34:30.139 --> 00:34:33.179
is equal to this
joint probability

00:34:33.179 --> 00:34:41.020
distribution of x, your image in
pixel space, and z, your latent.

00:34:41.020 --> 00:34:44.380
And you're just going
to integrate over

00:34:44.380 --> 00:34:50.420
all possible latent
representations.

00:34:50.420 --> 00:34:58.810
So this quantity is
nothing else than you're

00:34:58.810 --> 00:35:10.130
drawing from a prior, so p of
z, and then using your decoder

00:35:10.130 --> 00:35:15.090
to estimate x given z.

00:35:15.090 --> 00:35:21.770
So this quantity is the
integral of your prior times

00:35:21.770 --> 00:35:26.850
p theta of x given z, dz.

00:35:26.850 --> 00:35:30.330
P of z.

00:35:30.330 --> 00:35:30.990
Yeah, exactly.

00:35:30.990 --> 00:35:33.720
So the Gaussian.

00:35:33.720 --> 00:35:37.850
p theta x given z.

00:35:37.850 --> 00:35:42.210
Well, it's your model, your
model that you want to find.

00:35:42.210 --> 00:35:46.810
The problem is that
you need to compute

00:35:46.810 --> 00:35:53.570
this for all possible latent z.

00:35:53.570 --> 00:35:56.760
And z is still
high dimensional--

00:35:56.760 --> 00:35:59.720
high dimensional vector.

00:35:59.720 --> 00:36:05.160
So this, if you were to compute
this quantity for all z,

00:36:05.160 --> 00:36:08.040
it's actually not
something that you can do.

00:36:08.040 --> 00:36:13.600
Because you would have to
compute a lot of such quantities

00:36:13.600 --> 00:36:16.460
with given zs in order
to get a good estimate.

00:36:16.460 --> 00:36:20.360
And it's just not
something that you can do.

00:36:20.360 --> 00:36:29.000
So do you remember a similar
setting we had in lecture 1,

00:36:29.000 --> 00:36:31.560
which is the reason
why we're going

00:36:31.560 --> 00:36:36.080
to see how we can find
a convenient bound that

00:36:36.080 --> 00:36:38.200
could be maybe more tractable?

00:36:38.200 --> 00:36:41.480
Because so far, I
have not used the fact

00:36:41.480 --> 00:36:46.760
that we also have a way to
encode the input in order

00:36:46.760 --> 00:36:50.720
to get latents that are
more likely to be the ones

00:36:50.720 --> 00:36:54.150
that I want to consider here.

00:36:54.150 --> 00:37:00.390
So what I'm going to
do now is to find--

00:37:00.390 --> 00:37:04.530
so what I want to do first
is to maximize this quantity.

00:37:04.530 --> 00:37:09.390
So I want to find theta that
maximizes this quantity.

00:37:09.390 --> 00:37:12.590
So we saw just now that this
quantity is not something

00:37:12.590 --> 00:37:15.990
that I can actually compute.

00:37:15.990 --> 00:37:20.110
So what we're going
to do is to find

00:37:20.110 --> 00:37:23.550
a lower bound that
could be something

00:37:23.550 --> 00:37:26.150
that you could compute.

00:37:26.150 --> 00:37:28.910
And what we want to do is
to maximize that lower bound

00:37:28.910 --> 00:37:32.750
in the hope that it will
also maximize this in order

00:37:32.750 --> 00:37:37.630
to find our parameters theta.

00:37:37.630 --> 00:37:39.470
So let's do it.

00:37:39.470 --> 00:37:42.090
So you would have the following.

00:37:42.090 --> 00:37:44.230
So I'm going to just come here.

00:37:44.230 --> 00:37:50.510
I would just say that p of x
is equal to the integral of p

00:37:50.510 --> 00:37:58.000
of z times p theta of x given
z, which is just at that point,

00:37:58.000 --> 00:37:59.780
I'm going to use the
same trick that we

00:37:59.780 --> 00:38:09.940
did in lecture 1,
which is to involve

00:38:09.940 --> 00:38:12.180
the output of the encoder.

00:38:12.180 --> 00:38:22.440
And here, it is q phi of z given
x times q phi of z given x.

00:38:25.540 --> 00:38:27.320
So what does this represent?

00:38:27.320 --> 00:38:35.940
So this represents the
probability distribution

00:38:35.940 --> 00:38:42.460
of the zs that are likely
to occur for this given x.

00:38:42.460 --> 00:38:47.420
Because here, in the original
form, what you were doing

00:38:47.420 --> 00:38:52.610
was to just sum across all
possible latent representations

00:38:52.610 --> 00:38:57.090
out there, but there
are many of them.

00:38:57.090 --> 00:38:59.130
So it's like,
you're in the ocean,

00:38:59.130 --> 00:39:04.570
and someone tells you, OK, just
look through the whole ocean.

00:39:04.570 --> 00:39:10.970
But the thing is, only
a small subset of the zs

00:39:10.970 --> 00:39:13.450
are actually the
ones that you want.

00:39:13.450 --> 00:39:17.410
They're actually only
a subset of the zs that

00:39:17.410 --> 00:39:19.970
are the ones that are
responsible for your x

00:39:19.970 --> 00:39:25.730
to appear, which is the reason
why we're letting these q

00:39:25.730 --> 00:39:29.730
phi of z given x pop up.

00:39:29.730 --> 00:39:35.530
And this expression, so
this is equal to this.

00:39:35.530 --> 00:39:41.250
And this gets equal to just
the expectation of p of z times

00:39:41.250 --> 00:39:48.880
p theta of x given
z over q phi of z

00:39:48.880 --> 00:40:00.040
given x, where your z is
drawn from q phi of z given x.

00:40:00.040 --> 00:40:05.200
So why am I doing
this whole thing?

00:40:05.200 --> 00:40:11.320
Because what I want
to do is to take

00:40:11.320 --> 00:40:13.100
the log of this whole thing.

00:40:17.200 --> 00:40:23.440
I want to take the log
of this whole thing

00:40:23.440 --> 00:40:26.880
and do the same trick as
we did for the DPM, which

00:40:26.880 --> 00:40:31.800
is the Jensen's inequality.

00:40:31.800 --> 00:40:35.680
And I'm going to say
that it's the expectation

00:40:35.680 --> 00:40:38.740
of the log of this whole thing.

00:40:46.200 --> 00:40:51.910
So I'm taking the log of this
quantity, which I expressed

00:40:51.910 --> 00:40:58.590
as a function of the output
given by the encoder for which I

00:40:58.590 --> 00:41:02.350
can find a lower bound.

00:41:02.350 --> 00:41:07.070
And now, we're going to see that
this lower bound is actually

00:41:07.070 --> 00:41:08.530
something that is tractable.

00:41:12.510 --> 00:41:16.190
We're going to see it now.

00:41:16.190 --> 00:41:21.810
So what I'm going to do is to
just rewrite this expression.

00:41:21.810 --> 00:41:32.390
So we have-- so the expectation
of the log of the whole thing,

00:41:32.390 --> 00:41:39.110
which is equal to the
expectation of the log of p

00:41:39.110 --> 00:41:49.620
theta of x given z plus
the expectation of--

00:41:49.620 --> 00:41:51.600
so I'm going to be
a little bit smart.

00:41:51.600 --> 00:42:01.220
I will say minus the expectation
of the log of q phi of z

00:42:01.220 --> 00:42:08.320
given x over your
prior distribution.

00:42:12.660 --> 00:42:15.100
So here, what I
just did was I take

00:42:15.100 --> 00:42:22.620
the log of pz times p theta of x
given z over q phi of z given x.

00:42:22.620 --> 00:42:25.340
And what I did is I just--

00:42:25.340 --> 00:42:27.180
I used the property
of the log, which

00:42:27.180 --> 00:42:31.260
is logarithm of the
product, is the sum

00:42:31.260 --> 00:42:35.100
of the logarithm of the terms.

00:42:35.100 --> 00:42:37.820
So I had this one pop up.

00:42:37.820 --> 00:42:39.840
And this one, I just rearranged.

00:42:39.840 --> 00:42:42.140
I put the denominator
in the numerator,

00:42:42.140 --> 00:42:44.890
and I added the minus.

00:42:44.890 --> 00:42:48.170
And given that it's an
expectation for both

00:42:48.170 --> 00:42:55.210
of these that are over z drawn
from your encoder output, which

00:42:55.210 --> 00:43:04.050
is q phi of z given x,
this term is actually

00:43:04.050 --> 00:43:13.350
the KL divergence of q phi
of z given x and your prior.

00:43:19.290 --> 00:43:25.450
So what does this all mean?

00:43:25.450 --> 00:43:30.410
What this all means
is in order to find

00:43:30.410 --> 00:43:33.530
the theta that
maximizes this quantity,

00:43:33.530 --> 00:43:37.130
we derived a lower bound.

00:43:37.130 --> 00:43:39.870
That is something that
will see is tractable.

00:43:39.870 --> 00:43:46.760
So it's this expression, which
we have divided into two terms.

00:43:46.760 --> 00:43:49.960
So the first one is
expectation of the logarithm

00:43:49.960 --> 00:43:52.440
of p theta of x given z.

00:43:52.440 --> 00:43:54.980
And the other one
is a KL divergence.

00:43:57.880 --> 00:44:00.360
So we're going to see that
these two terms are actually

00:44:00.360 --> 00:44:03.400
quite interpretable.

00:44:03.400 --> 00:44:09.920
The first one is actually
a reconstruction term.

00:44:09.920 --> 00:44:15.120
Because if you remember,
p theta of x given z

00:44:15.120 --> 00:44:20.360
is a normal distribution
with fixed variance.

00:44:20.360 --> 00:44:24.200
So we know the probability
density analytically.

00:44:24.200 --> 00:44:28.200
So if you take the
log of the probability

00:44:28.200 --> 00:44:32.080
density of a Gaussian,
you actually end up

00:44:32.080 --> 00:44:37.420
with something super simple,
which is the norm of x.

00:44:37.420 --> 00:44:42.590
So the input and the
reconstructed outputs.

00:44:42.590 --> 00:44:46.170
And then the second
term is actually a term.

00:44:46.170 --> 00:44:47.910
So back to your question.

00:44:47.910 --> 00:44:53.230
It is a term that penalizes
the distribution coming

00:44:53.230 --> 00:45:00.670
from your encoder being far from
this fixed prior distribution.

00:45:00.670 --> 00:45:04.270
So loss functions usually
have a coefficient

00:45:04.270 --> 00:45:06.990
in front of this
term to penalize

00:45:06.990 --> 00:45:11.990
more or less the fact
of being far or close

00:45:11.990 --> 00:45:14.030
from this prior distribution.

00:45:14.030 --> 00:45:17.390
And depending on the
coefficient there,

00:45:17.390 --> 00:45:21.230
you're going to have
either something that

00:45:21.230 --> 00:45:26.270
will look like a normal
distribution or not.

00:45:26.270 --> 00:45:30.050
So that is the part of your loss
that will allow you to do that.

00:45:32.830 --> 00:45:34.630
I'm looking at the time.

00:45:34.630 --> 00:45:36.790
I'm not super in
advance, but I'm

00:45:36.790 --> 00:45:38.340
going to just pause
for one minute

00:45:38.340 --> 00:45:40.940
if you have any questions.

00:45:40.940 --> 00:45:44.060
So the question is we're pushing
the distribution of the encoder

00:45:44.060 --> 00:45:46.560
to be more of the
normal distribution.

00:45:46.560 --> 00:45:51.160
So we want to do that to
normalize the latent space,

00:45:51.160 --> 00:45:53.580
but we don't want
to do that too much.

00:45:53.580 --> 00:45:58.300
Otherwise, so the prior
distribution is p of z.

00:45:58.300 --> 00:46:01.140
It doesn't care what x is.

00:46:01.140 --> 00:46:05.340
And in other words, if you push
it too much towards something

00:46:05.340 --> 00:46:11.660
that is just static,
it will just not

00:46:11.660 --> 00:46:14.052
consider the input at all.

00:46:14.052 --> 00:46:14.760
It will collapse.

00:46:14.760 --> 00:46:18.140
Yeah, so it's called
posterior collapse.

00:46:18.140 --> 00:46:20.760
So just one other question,
and then I'll move on.

00:46:20.760 --> 00:46:21.260
Yeah.

00:46:21.260 --> 00:46:23.940
So the question is--
so we had this image

00:46:23.940 --> 00:46:27.020
with the little
spikiness and that

00:46:27.020 --> 00:46:29.840
was the autoencoder, the
standard autoencoder part.

00:46:29.840 --> 00:46:33.420
This one is actually structured
in the latent space in a way

00:46:33.420 --> 00:46:37.590
that makes it more smooth, more
well put together, and yes.

00:46:40.370 --> 00:46:43.890
So I'm going to speed
up a little bit.

00:46:43.890 --> 00:46:46.370
Let's go through our checklist.

00:46:46.370 --> 00:46:49.970
Tractable dimension, yes.

00:46:49.970 --> 00:46:53.730
Compact representation, yes.

00:46:53.730 --> 00:46:59.050
Meaningful representation, yes.

00:46:59.050 --> 00:47:02.650
Oops, truthful representation.

00:47:02.650 --> 00:47:03.970
No.

00:47:03.970 --> 00:47:04.470
Wait.

00:47:04.470 --> 00:47:06.250
Why is that?

00:47:06.250 --> 00:47:13.290
So these models, they actually
end up reconstructing the image

00:47:13.290 --> 00:47:15.850
in an imperfect way.

00:47:15.850 --> 00:47:21.770
And what we actually obtain
are more blurry images.

00:47:21.770 --> 00:47:24.890
And so I'm hoping that
in the next nine minutes,

00:47:24.890 --> 00:47:28.050
we're going to see
why that is the case

00:47:28.050 --> 00:47:32.050
and how we can solve it.

00:47:32.050 --> 00:47:35.820
So first of all,
let's look at the loss

00:47:35.820 --> 00:47:37.320
function that we
have derived, which

00:47:37.320 --> 00:47:44.640
is composed of a reconstruction
loss and a regularization loss.

00:47:44.640 --> 00:47:45.920
So the reconstruction loss.

00:47:45.920 --> 00:47:48.200
What is it?

00:47:48.200 --> 00:47:49.900
We have not derived it here.

00:47:49.900 --> 00:47:53.840
But I am telling you
that if you derive it,

00:47:53.840 --> 00:48:00.800
it is the pixel wise
L2 between the input

00:48:00.800 --> 00:48:05.880
and the reconstructed output,
the reconstructed input, sorry.

00:48:05.880 --> 00:48:12.040
And so what that means is if the
generation is a little bit off

00:48:12.040 --> 00:48:18.560
by a few pixels, it will
actually get very penalized.

00:48:18.560 --> 00:48:20.940
Because the distance
is pixel wise.

00:48:23.520 --> 00:48:27.400
So what ends up
happening is the model

00:48:27.400 --> 00:48:32.550
tends to produce values
that are more averaged,

00:48:32.550 --> 00:48:37.310
which produces this blurriness
sensation that you're seeing.

00:48:37.310 --> 00:48:42.350
But then you will tell me, well,
Afshin, the standard autoencoder

00:48:42.350 --> 00:48:44.930
also had that loss.

00:48:44.930 --> 00:48:47.590
So why didn't it happen there?

00:48:47.590 --> 00:48:49.890
Well, the fact is, it
also happened there,

00:48:49.890 --> 00:48:53.950
but it's even more
pronounced here

00:48:53.950 --> 00:49:00.010
because of the structure of
the model that we have defined,

00:49:00.010 --> 00:49:04.590
which is that the latent
representation is not

00:49:04.590 --> 00:49:10.030
a one to one mapping with
the input x, it is not.

00:49:10.030 --> 00:49:14.870
Instead, what we have
said is a given input x

00:49:14.870 --> 00:49:17.470
maps to a probability
distribution,

00:49:17.470 --> 00:49:19.670
like a set of
probable points that

00:49:19.670 --> 00:49:25.510
could be actually latent
representations of this x.

00:49:25.510 --> 00:49:27.990
So the model will
introduce this uncertainty

00:49:27.990 --> 00:49:33.860
even more in the reconstruction
that it will output.

00:49:33.860 --> 00:49:40.700
And here, what we want
to do is to combat

00:49:40.700 --> 00:49:43.400
that blurriness sensation.

00:49:43.400 --> 00:49:48.820
Because here, if we were to
perform diffusion in the latent

00:49:48.820 --> 00:49:55.900
space, and if we were to decode
the final output in the latent

00:49:55.900 --> 00:49:59.140
space, the last
thing that we want

00:49:59.140 --> 00:50:01.860
is for our output to be blurry.

00:50:01.860 --> 00:50:05.380
We do not want that.

00:50:05.380 --> 00:50:09.860
So now, we're going to
see a couple of strategies

00:50:09.860 --> 00:50:15.340
that will allow us to combat
that blurriness artifact

00:50:15.340 --> 00:50:17.700
that we're seeing.

00:50:17.700 --> 00:50:22.700
And the first one is going to
be about a kind of loss that's

00:50:22.700 --> 00:50:25.420
called the perceptual loss.

00:50:25.420 --> 00:50:28.380
I'm going to say
exactly what that means.

00:50:28.380 --> 00:50:32.030
So let's imagine you
have your image x.

00:50:34.610 --> 00:50:39.810
The intuition here is we don't
want the reconstructed output

00:50:39.810 --> 00:50:42.610
to be compared with
the input pixel

00:50:42.610 --> 00:50:47.210
to pixel because it does not
allow for little shifting

00:50:47.210 --> 00:50:49.570
here and there.

00:50:49.570 --> 00:50:55.170
So what we want instead is to
be able to compare the input

00:50:55.170 --> 00:50:58.610
and the reconstruction
more in terms

00:50:58.610 --> 00:51:03.250
of similarity, that something
that looks similar to you

00:51:03.250 --> 00:51:08.450
as a human,
perceptually similar.

00:51:08.450 --> 00:51:10.170
So how can you do that?

00:51:10.170 --> 00:51:14.890
Well, there are some
models out there

00:51:14.890 --> 00:51:19.890
that there are convolutional
based, for instance,

00:51:19.890 --> 00:51:24.730
that actually extract
features from these images

00:51:24.730 --> 00:51:26.960
in their layers.

00:51:26.960 --> 00:51:32.120
And here, we refer
them as feature maps.

00:51:32.120 --> 00:51:35.280
And one good thing-- one good
thing about these feature maps

00:51:35.280 --> 00:51:41.960
is that they represent
things from this image that

00:51:41.960 --> 00:51:44.760
have more of a
spatial invariance

00:51:44.760 --> 00:51:48.400
compared to the original image.

00:51:48.400 --> 00:51:50.480
So what does that mean?

00:51:50.480 --> 00:51:55.680
That means that if you have
two images that are slightly

00:51:55.680 --> 00:51:58.880
shifted, slightly
different, then

00:51:58.880 --> 00:52:04.160
their feature maps will
actually not be that different.

00:52:04.160 --> 00:52:10.160
So the idea here is instead
of comparing the pixel based

00:52:10.160 --> 00:52:13.720
images one to the
other, we're going

00:52:13.720 --> 00:52:18.800
to compare their representation,
their feature maps

00:52:18.800 --> 00:52:21.520
one to another.

00:52:21.520 --> 00:52:23.520
And I'm not sure if
you've heard that loss,

00:52:23.520 --> 00:52:27.030
but there is a loss
that's called LPIPS, so

00:52:27.030 --> 00:52:31.630
Learn Perceptual Image
Patch Similarity.

00:52:31.630 --> 00:52:35.990
So it is a loss or it is
a metric I should say,

00:52:35.990 --> 00:52:40.070
that is composed of the
difference between the feature

00:52:40.070 --> 00:52:46.430
maps of two images, and that are
weighted by a coefficient, which

00:52:46.430 --> 00:52:49.630
is here WL.

00:52:49.630 --> 00:52:52.390
There are coefficients
that are tuned

00:52:52.390 --> 00:52:54.870
by the authors of
this paper in order

00:52:54.870 --> 00:53:02.070
to match what humans consider
as being perceptually similar.

00:53:02.070 --> 00:53:05.450
So long story short-- so by
the way, the formula is scary,

00:53:05.450 --> 00:53:07.210
but it's not that big of a deal.

00:53:07.210 --> 00:53:10.410
We don't care about
the whole thing.

00:53:10.410 --> 00:53:13.790
What we care about is
that we're not comparing

00:53:13.790 --> 00:53:16.530
images in the pixel space.

00:53:16.530 --> 00:53:22.590
We're comparing images
between their feature maps.

00:53:22.590 --> 00:53:24.940
And what we're saying here
is that the feature maps

00:53:24.940 --> 00:53:31.860
of two images better reflect
their structure, their details,

00:53:31.860 --> 00:53:37.220
and are less sensitive
to spatial differences.

00:53:37.220 --> 00:53:38.920
So that is one strategy.

00:53:41.700 --> 00:53:46.700
So one thing to note is that
we would weight this loss

00:53:46.700 --> 00:53:48.460
by some coefficients.

00:53:48.460 --> 00:53:51.960
You could think, OK, this loss
is answering all our needs.

00:53:51.960 --> 00:53:55.900
Let's have the weight for this
part of the loss be super high.

00:53:55.900 --> 00:53:57.580
Well, if you do
that, actually, there

00:53:57.580 --> 00:53:59.940
are some bad things
that also happen.

00:53:59.940 --> 00:54:04.380
So we have what we call
checkerboard artifacts, which

00:54:04.380 --> 00:54:07.540
as you can tell is like
a grid like artifact

00:54:07.540 --> 00:54:11.120
in the image that is
similar to this one.

00:54:11.120 --> 00:54:15.480
So you don't want to have
too big of a weight either.

00:54:18.860 --> 00:54:20.100
So that's one.

00:54:20.100 --> 00:54:24.090
And then a second strategy
to combat blurriness

00:54:24.090 --> 00:54:27.410
is what we're going to see now.

00:54:27.410 --> 00:54:33.730
So the problem that we have
is that the decoder of the VA

00:54:33.730 --> 00:54:38.290
is outputting an
image that is blurry.

00:54:38.290 --> 00:54:43.890
So one idea could be
to ask another model

00:54:43.890 --> 00:54:46.210
to tell you whether
the input image, so

00:54:46.210 --> 00:54:50.070
in this case, the blurry
image is a real image or not?

00:54:52.650 --> 00:54:55.970
And based on that,
what we would do

00:54:55.970 --> 00:55:00.730
is incentivize this
other network called

00:55:00.730 --> 00:55:04.850
the discriminator, to
distinguish outputs coming

00:55:04.850 --> 00:55:09.450
from the decoder compared to
real images, which would then

00:55:09.450 --> 00:55:14.130
flag blurry images
as being fake.

00:55:14.130 --> 00:55:18.650
And we would
penalize the decoder

00:55:18.650 --> 00:55:22.000
for generating images
that would be flagged

00:55:22.000 --> 00:55:25.920
as fake by the discriminator.

00:55:25.920 --> 00:55:29.040
So have you heard
of GANs before?

00:55:29.040 --> 00:55:31.580
So we don't have time to
talk about them in detail,

00:55:31.580 --> 00:55:35.260
but I think this slide is
summing up what is happening.

00:55:35.260 --> 00:55:40.840
So you have two models that
are competing with one another.

00:55:40.840 --> 00:55:45.200
The decoder that wants
to produce images that

00:55:45.200 --> 00:55:49.720
the discriminator cannot
distinguish from being fake,

00:55:49.720 --> 00:55:53.400
and the discriminator that wants
to distinguish the output from

00:55:53.400 --> 00:56:00.920
the decoder compared to
images from real life.

00:56:00.920 --> 00:56:05.000
And what this will
do is incentivize

00:56:05.000 --> 00:56:09.680
the decoder to produce
more realistic images.

00:56:09.680 --> 00:56:16.280
And in doing so, it will help
with removing that blurriness.

00:56:16.280 --> 00:56:20.430
So that is called
adversarial loss.

00:56:20.430 --> 00:56:25.810
So what we saw was that the
original loss of the VA,

00:56:25.810 --> 00:56:33.610
which we derived here, led to
outputs that were too blurry.

00:56:33.610 --> 00:56:36.410
It was fulfilling all the
wish list that we had,

00:56:36.410 --> 00:56:41.510
but it was not leading to a
truthful reconstruction, which

00:56:41.510 --> 00:56:47.070
is the reason why we saw
two different strategies

00:56:47.070 --> 00:56:50.750
to add to our loss,
to counterbalance

00:56:50.750 --> 00:56:55.110
the tendency for the model
to produce blurry outputs.

00:56:55.110 --> 00:57:01.110
So these are the perceptual
loss and the adversarial loss.

00:57:01.110 --> 00:57:04.430
So before giving
it to Sherwin, I

00:57:04.430 --> 00:57:06.830
could not let you
without telling you

00:57:06.830 --> 00:57:10.630
how this fits together
with the diffusion models.

00:57:10.630 --> 00:57:15.750
So in particular, the reason
why we did this whole thing

00:57:15.750 --> 00:57:20.420
is for us to use our
diffusion flow matching

00:57:20.420 --> 00:57:25.380
model not in the pixel space,
but in the latent space.

00:57:25.380 --> 00:57:29.060
Because computationally
it's just cheaper

00:57:29.060 --> 00:57:31.740
and it's able to scale.

00:57:31.740 --> 00:57:35.060
So now, we're going to see
what our strategy for training

00:57:35.060 --> 00:57:37.620
and inference is.

00:57:37.620 --> 00:57:40.660
So for training, what
we do is we first train

00:57:40.660 --> 00:57:45.900
the VA exactly like we saw.

00:57:45.900 --> 00:57:50.220
And then what we're going to do
is to train our image generation

00:57:50.220 --> 00:57:53.020
model, like using for
instance flow matching,

00:57:53.020 --> 00:57:57.620
in the latent space of the VA.

00:57:57.620 --> 00:58:00.300
And we're going to
see how we do that.

00:58:00.300 --> 00:58:04.060
So let's suppose we have an
image from our target data

00:58:04.060 --> 00:58:06.940
distribution.

00:58:06.940 --> 00:58:09.740
We pass it through the encoder.

00:58:09.740 --> 00:58:13.280
Our encoder gives
us two quantities.

00:58:13.280 --> 00:58:16.090
So the mean and the
standard deviation.

00:58:16.090 --> 00:58:20.170
And what we do is we sample
from that distribution

00:58:20.170 --> 00:58:23.770
to get our latent
representation.

00:58:23.770 --> 00:58:27.810
And then what happens
is in our latent space,

00:58:27.810 --> 00:58:31.530
so we hopefully have a
smoother latent space.

00:58:31.530 --> 00:58:36.170
We have our z1, which
corresponds to the clean image.

00:58:36.170 --> 00:58:41.970
We sample from the
noisy data distribution.

00:58:41.970 --> 00:58:45.110
And here, I'm just illustrating
the case of flow matching,

00:58:45.110 --> 00:58:49.970
we have zt, which is a weighted
combination of the two.

00:58:49.970 --> 00:58:52.650
And what we do is we
optimize for, let's say,

00:58:52.650 --> 00:58:56.190
our velocity like the l2 loss.

00:58:56.190 --> 00:58:58.410
We'll do that.

00:58:58.410 --> 00:59:00.850
This is for training.

00:59:00.850 --> 00:59:03.570
So how about inference?

00:59:03.570 --> 00:59:08.930
Well, let's assume that you
have trained your model.

00:59:08.930 --> 00:59:13.560
Then now, what you want to
do is to generate new images.

00:59:13.560 --> 00:59:18.840
So what you're going to
do is to first sample

00:59:18.840 --> 00:59:21.940
from an easy distribution
in the latent space.

00:59:21.940 --> 00:59:25.000
So for instance, Gaussian noise.

00:59:25.000 --> 00:59:29.680
And you're going to
use, for instance flow

00:59:29.680 --> 00:59:32.880
matching, the velocity
that you have learned

00:59:32.880 --> 00:59:37.880
to solve it numerically and
arrive, at let's say a point

00:59:37.880 --> 00:59:43.680
z1, which is the latent
representation of your end

00:59:43.680 --> 00:59:45.800
trajectory.

00:59:45.800 --> 00:59:47.640
And what we're going
to do is to use

00:59:47.640 --> 00:59:53.848
the VAE decoder to decode that
output back to pixel space.

00:59:53.848 --> 00:59:55.140
So let's see how it looks like.

00:59:55.140 --> 00:59:58.360
So this is your latent
space that you have learned.

00:59:58.360 --> 01:00:00.940
You sample from the
noisy distribution

01:00:00.940 --> 01:00:03.280
in that latent space.

01:00:03.280 --> 01:00:08.400
You solve numerically the ODE
using the learned vector fields

01:00:08.400 --> 01:00:10.600
that we've trained right before.

01:00:10.600 --> 01:00:12.710
You obtain z1.

01:00:12.710 --> 01:00:16.430
The problem is, z1 to us,
it doesn't mean anything

01:00:16.430 --> 01:00:19.990
because it's not
in a pixel space.

01:00:19.990 --> 01:00:22.350
So what you do is
you take that z1,

01:00:22.350 --> 01:00:26.790
you pass it through the
decoder, and you obtain

01:00:26.790 --> 01:00:31.310
the image in pixel space.

01:00:31.310 --> 01:00:36.350
So to conclude, what
we've seen is a way

01:00:36.350 --> 01:00:42.190
to find a latent space that
is of lower dimension, that

01:00:42.190 --> 01:00:49.670
is smoother, and that
is also able to preserve

01:00:49.670 --> 01:00:52.550
the details of your image.

01:00:52.550 --> 01:00:55.630
And so in particular,
we say that the encoder

01:00:55.630 --> 01:00:59.790
acts as a low pass filter.

01:00:59.790 --> 01:01:06.910
Because what it does represents
those images mostly based

01:01:06.910 --> 01:01:10.780
on their semantic similarity.

01:01:10.780 --> 01:01:12.840
So this is actually
something we have not seen.

01:01:12.840 --> 01:01:20.140
But for a diffusion model
to find all the details

01:01:20.140 --> 01:01:22.180
in its exploration
is much harder

01:01:22.180 --> 01:01:25.100
than finding something
that is semantically

01:01:25.100 --> 01:01:27.780
what it wants to generate.

01:01:27.780 --> 01:01:32.020
So this is the reason why
we're giving the decoder

01:01:32.020 --> 01:01:36.860
the task of conveying all
the low level details, all

01:01:36.860 --> 01:01:40.540
the perceptual elements.

01:01:40.540 --> 01:01:43.940
And the role of the decoder
is to take that latent

01:01:43.940 --> 01:01:48.300
representation and to
make it an output that

01:01:48.300 --> 01:01:51.100
is full of details,
that is realistic,

01:01:51.100 --> 01:01:54.420
and that is matching
what we want.

01:01:54.420 --> 01:01:57.420
And for that reason,
you have the encoder

01:01:57.420 --> 01:02:02.180
and the decoder, which you
would think is symmetric.

01:02:02.180 --> 01:02:07.260
But in practice, the VAEs
that are used in conjunction

01:02:07.260 --> 01:02:12.130
with diffusion models are such
that the decoder is actually

01:02:12.130 --> 01:02:14.870
bigger than the encoder.

01:02:20.435 --> 01:02:21.310
Does that make sense?

01:02:21.310 --> 01:02:23.670
So we don't have a lot of time.

01:02:23.670 --> 01:02:27.010
So one thing that
I put down there

01:02:27.010 --> 01:02:30.570
is a longer form
version of everything

01:02:30.570 --> 01:02:35.130
I mentioned in some very
interesting articles, which

01:02:35.130 --> 01:02:38.730
I recommend reading,
but not mandatory

01:02:38.730 --> 01:02:44.410
as part of this class, which is
some results from flux 2, which

01:02:44.410 --> 01:02:49.610
is a text to image model,
and the LDM paper, the Latent

01:02:49.610 --> 01:02:54.730
Diffusion Model paper that is
at the bottom of the slide.

01:02:54.730 --> 01:02:56.890
Yeah.

01:02:56.890 --> 01:02:59.970
So the question
is, what should we

01:02:59.970 --> 01:03:02.690
think of the role of the
encoder and the decoder?

01:03:02.690 --> 01:03:05.250
So at the end of
the day, what you

01:03:05.250 --> 01:03:09.400
want is to have an easy
time generating images,

01:03:09.400 --> 01:03:10.920
at the end of the day.

01:03:10.920 --> 01:03:14.480
And what you want is
to not have too high

01:03:14.480 --> 01:03:18.600
of a computational burden for
your diffusion model to operate.

01:03:18.600 --> 01:03:21.800
So what you want
is a latent space

01:03:21.800 --> 01:03:27.240
that is low in dimensionality,
and that is easy for you

01:03:27.240 --> 01:03:29.360
to learn.

01:03:29.360 --> 01:03:31.980
So what does easy for
you to learn mean?

01:03:31.980 --> 01:03:35.940
It means having a space
that is not super spiky,

01:03:35.940 --> 01:03:39.000
but that is more smooth.

01:03:39.000 --> 01:03:45.760
And in particular, in order
for you to obtain such a space,

01:03:45.760 --> 01:03:50.120
it is actually something
that in practice, something

01:03:50.120 --> 01:03:54.720
that you can obtain if you
focus on the semantic meaning

01:03:54.720 --> 01:03:59.200
of these images, as opposed
to capturing all the low level

01:03:59.200 --> 01:03:59.700
details.

01:03:59.700 --> 01:04:02.160
So in other words,
what I'm saying

01:04:02.160 --> 01:04:08.210
is, even if two images are
perceptually not very similar,

01:04:08.210 --> 01:04:10.750
but if they represent
the same thing,

01:04:10.750 --> 01:04:15.030
what we want for these images
is to be located not too

01:04:15.030 --> 01:04:18.030
far from one another
in the latent space.

01:04:18.030 --> 01:04:23.830
So we want the encoder to mostly
capture the semantic meaning.

01:04:23.830 --> 01:04:27.870
So the encoder, what we want is
to make the job of the diffusion

01:04:27.870 --> 01:04:29.550
model easy.

01:04:29.550 --> 01:04:31.470
And so this is the learnability.

01:04:31.470 --> 01:04:34.910
You will see in those
paper, learnability.

01:04:34.910 --> 01:04:39.150
So once you have images that
are semantically similar,

01:04:39.150 --> 01:04:44.470
that are close to one another,
you do your diffusion process.

01:04:44.470 --> 01:04:46.890
You obtain, let's say
a point that you say,

01:04:46.890 --> 01:04:49.510
OK, this is the
latent representation

01:04:49.510 --> 01:04:53.750
that I want to have
in the pixel space.

01:04:53.750 --> 01:04:56.510
So now, the job
of your decoder is

01:04:56.510 --> 01:05:00.550
to make sure that all the
details that you want to have

01:05:00.550 --> 01:05:03.620
in this final image are there.

01:05:03.620 --> 01:05:07.820
So in that sense, in
contrast to the encoder,

01:05:07.820 --> 01:05:13.660
the decoders job is to make sure
all the perceptual details are

01:05:13.660 --> 01:05:18.420
present in the final decoded
image in the pixel space.

01:05:18.420 --> 01:05:21.740
So in that sense, you have
these two different roles

01:05:21.740 --> 01:05:23.360
of the encoder and the decoder.

01:05:23.360 --> 01:05:27.900
So you want your diffusion model
to have an easy time learning

01:05:27.900 --> 01:05:30.660
this thing, and you
want a space that

01:05:30.660 --> 01:05:34.180
has some semantic
information, which is what

01:05:34.180 --> 01:05:36.780
I mentioned the encoder can do.

01:05:36.780 --> 01:05:38.540
But what you want, at
the end of the day,

01:05:38.540 --> 01:05:42.860
is to have an image that
contains the details that you

01:05:42.860 --> 01:05:46.300
want, and that part
is mostly something

01:05:46.300 --> 01:05:52.380
that decoder can help with
in the decoding stage.

01:05:52.380 --> 01:05:55.540
OK, I'm 10 minutes over
time, but with that, I'll

01:05:55.540 --> 01:05:58.060
give it to Sherwin.

01:05:58.060 --> 01:05:59.860
Thank you, Afshin.

01:05:59.860 --> 01:06:02.490
So now, we're going
to see together

01:06:02.490 --> 01:06:05.530
how to represent the
conditions that we

01:06:05.530 --> 01:06:08.610
have at the input of
this generation models.

01:06:08.610 --> 01:06:11.090
So the first kind of
condition that we can have

01:06:11.090 --> 01:06:13.810
is the text one.

01:06:13.810 --> 01:06:18.530
So the text world, when it
comes to representing embeddings

01:06:18.530 --> 01:06:20.370
has a long history.

01:06:20.370 --> 01:06:23.170
And I'm not going to go through
the whole history of how

01:06:23.170 --> 01:06:24.310
we represent embeddings.

01:06:24.310 --> 01:06:27.090
I'm just going to focus
on how people do it today.

01:06:27.090 --> 01:06:33.210
And the way they do it is with
transformer-based architectures.

01:06:33.210 --> 01:06:40.770
So let's first see how we can
decompose the input sentence.

01:06:40.770 --> 01:06:43.810
So we have this concept
called tokenization,

01:06:43.810 --> 01:06:48.370
which is the practice of
decomposing any piece of text

01:06:48.370 --> 01:06:51.490
into atomic components.

01:06:51.490 --> 01:06:53.490
So you can have
something that operates

01:06:53.490 --> 01:06:58.610
at an arbitrary level,
word level, subword level

01:06:58.610 --> 01:07:00.440
or character level.

01:07:00.440 --> 01:07:05.000
And a software level
is usually algorithms

01:07:05.000 --> 01:07:09.880
that learn how to best decompose
the tokens with respect

01:07:09.880 --> 01:07:14.520
to the data by
focusing a vocabulary

01:07:14.520 --> 01:07:19.080
budget on those tokens
that most frequently occur.

01:07:19.080 --> 01:07:23.080
And the tokenization technique
that's most used these days

01:07:23.080 --> 01:07:24.720
is subword.

01:07:24.720 --> 01:07:28.120
But for simplicity,
the one that you're

01:07:28.120 --> 01:07:30.900
going to see in the slides
is more towards arbitrary,

01:07:30.900 --> 01:07:34.320
but it's just for
representation purposes.

01:07:34.320 --> 01:07:38.760
So who knows about transformers?

01:07:38.760 --> 01:07:40.360
OK, so most of you.

01:07:40.360 --> 01:07:42.120
So there is a sister
course that talks

01:07:42.120 --> 01:07:44.180
about transformers at length.

01:07:44.180 --> 01:07:46.340
I'm not going to talk about
transformers at length,

01:07:46.340 --> 01:07:49.200
but just give the main
pieces of information

01:07:49.200 --> 01:07:50.840
that you should know.

01:07:50.840 --> 01:07:56.680
So it is an encoder,
decoder architecture

01:07:56.680 --> 01:08:00.750
that is centered on the
concept of extension.

01:08:00.750 --> 01:08:03.530
And it came out in 2017.

01:08:03.530 --> 01:08:06.230
And most of the language
models that you see today

01:08:06.230 --> 01:08:08.470
are based off of it.

01:08:08.470 --> 01:08:11.070
Even vision models,
you have many of them

01:08:11.070 --> 01:08:14.470
that are based on
the transformer.

01:08:14.470 --> 01:08:18.510
And the good thing about
it that made it work well

01:08:18.510 --> 01:08:20.910
is that it's
inductive biases are

01:08:20.910 --> 01:08:22.910
based on the concept
of attention that

01:08:22.910 --> 01:08:25.630
proved to be generalizable.

01:08:25.630 --> 01:08:30.830
And let's focus now on
this concept of attention.

01:08:30.830 --> 01:08:35.710
So if we had to summarize in
a single sentence what it is,

01:08:35.710 --> 01:08:38.750
it is the practice
of representing

01:08:38.750 --> 01:08:44.590
a given piece of input as a
function of all the others.

01:08:44.590 --> 01:08:47.550
And here, with the
example, a cute Teddy bear

01:08:47.550 --> 01:08:50.510
is reading, if we
want to represent

01:08:50.510 --> 01:08:53.870
in the space of
attention the token Teddy

01:08:53.870 --> 01:08:58.340
bear, what we do is that we have
embeddings for all these tokens.

01:08:58.340 --> 01:09:01.700
You have learnable matrices
that will turn them

01:09:01.700 --> 01:09:08.100
into the space of queries,
q, keys, k, and values, v.

01:09:08.100 --> 01:09:13.899
And what you're going to learn
is a distribution of keys--

01:09:13.899 --> 01:09:17.220
of this key over keys.

01:09:17.220 --> 01:09:19.340
And the weights
that you obtain are

01:09:19.340 --> 01:09:23.620
going to be those that you place
on the corresponding values.

01:09:23.620 --> 01:09:27.540
So in summary, you're able to
represent the embedding of Teddy

01:09:27.540 --> 01:09:33.460
bear with respect to the others
in this sentence in that way.

01:09:33.460 --> 01:09:37.819
And you can write this
down in matrix form, which

01:09:37.819 --> 01:09:42.580
is this softmax term that
represents this probability

01:09:42.580 --> 01:09:43.939
distribution.

01:09:43.939 --> 01:09:46.380
So the square root of
DK is just a normalizing

01:09:46.380 --> 01:09:51.080
constant that prevents the
sum from becoming too large.

01:09:51.080 --> 01:09:55.290
So the dimension of
these queries and keys.

01:09:55.290 --> 01:09:58.490
And multiplied by the
value, which carries

01:09:58.490 --> 01:10:02.690
the embedding of the values.

01:10:02.690 --> 01:10:05.850
So you can find
this attention layer

01:10:05.850 --> 01:10:07.910
at three different locations.

01:10:07.910 --> 01:10:12.550
So two of them are
self-attention layers.

01:10:12.550 --> 01:10:14.530
So in the encoder
and another one

01:10:14.530 --> 01:10:18.410
masked self-attention,
which conveys the fact

01:10:18.410 --> 01:10:21.210
that tokens can
attend only on those

01:10:21.210 --> 01:10:23.290
that were predicted before.

01:10:23.290 --> 01:10:27.830
And then you have another
layer, where attention occurs,

01:10:27.830 --> 01:10:30.410
which is called cross
attention, which

01:10:30.410 --> 01:10:37.090
will make these queries
attend on other encoded keys

01:10:37.090 --> 01:10:39.050
and values.

01:10:39.050 --> 01:10:41.450
So this is the high level.

01:10:41.450 --> 01:10:45.050
And in just a few words,
the main components

01:10:45.050 --> 01:10:50.570
is that your input tokens
are converted into embeddings

01:10:50.570 --> 01:10:52.200
with a learnable layer.

01:10:52.200 --> 01:10:55.280
You add some
information regarding

01:10:55.280 --> 01:10:57.320
the position of your tokens.

01:10:57.320 --> 01:11:00.880
Then you make it through or
go through an encoder that

01:11:00.880 --> 01:11:03.560
will apply this
concept of attention

01:11:03.560 --> 01:11:08.200
and also add on top of it
some learnable mechanism

01:11:08.200 --> 01:11:15.280
to add some complexity for each
tokens representation, which

01:11:15.280 --> 01:11:19.920
is those feedforward network.

01:11:19.920 --> 01:11:22.520
And then the decoder level,
when you want to generate,

01:11:22.520 --> 01:11:28.240
let's say a sentence, you start
with some placeholder token

01:11:28.240 --> 01:11:30.280
that you--

01:11:30.280 --> 01:11:32.800
for which you add position
embeddings as well,

01:11:32.800 --> 01:11:34.920
that you self-attention
and then perform

01:11:34.920 --> 01:11:38.880
cross-attention with respect
to your encoders tokens.

01:11:38.880 --> 01:11:42.360
And then you also
have feedforward there

01:11:42.360 --> 01:11:46.920
to finally obtain your output.

01:11:46.920 --> 01:11:50.550
So I'm telling you
all that to mention

01:11:50.550 --> 01:11:55.710
that there are locations where
embeddings can be extracted out

01:11:55.710 --> 01:11:56.450
of all of these.

01:11:56.450 --> 01:11:59.230
So if you use the encoder
part of the network,

01:11:59.230 --> 01:12:05.230
you can find the ends of the
encoders, a location where

01:12:05.230 --> 01:12:11.150
you have an encoded meaning
of your inputs tokens.

01:12:11.150 --> 01:12:13.390
And this is typically
one of those locations,

01:12:13.390 --> 01:12:19.990
where you could say these are
the embeddings of my input.

01:12:19.990 --> 01:12:21.010
OK, great.

01:12:21.010 --> 01:12:24.530
So now, doing the
same for images.

01:12:24.530 --> 01:12:28.270
Let's say you have a condition
that is based on images.

01:12:28.270 --> 01:12:31.190
One thing that is pretty
nice is that everything

01:12:31.190 --> 01:12:36.150
that we mentioned just now
can be reproduced with images.

01:12:36.150 --> 01:12:38.950
So all these token
representations

01:12:38.950 --> 01:12:40.510
are just numbers.

01:12:40.510 --> 01:12:46.470
And you could well
represent these numbers.

01:12:46.470 --> 01:12:49.540
You can associate them
with patches of images.

01:12:49.540 --> 01:12:57.580
And this is what the ViT vision
transformer paper does, which

01:12:57.580 --> 01:12:59.420
follows the same principle.

01:12:59.420 --> 01:13:03.280
But instead of learning
embeddings on tokens,

01:13:03.280 --> 01:13:07.660
you learn embeddings of
on patches of images.

01:13:07.660 --> 01:13:10.020
So everything stays the same.

01:13:10.020 --> 01:13:19.180
And you can perform tasks, such
as image classification with it.

01:13:19.180 --> 01:13:21.980
And something that I haven't
done in the tech space

01:13:21.980 --> 01:13:25.000
that we can do here together
in this image world,

01:13:25.000 --> 01:13:27.900
is to see how these
pieces come together

01:13:27.900 --> 01:13:32.500
in this example of a
Teddy bear reading a book.

01:13:32.500 --> 01:13:36.260
So here, you decompose your
image into these patches

01:13:36.260 --> 01:13:38.460
that we mentioned.

01:13:38.460 --> 01:13:42.300
Each of these patches,
you have a learnable layer

01:13:42.300 --> 01:13:44.820
that gives you an initial
representation of what

01:13:44.820 --> 01:13:45.860
they mean.

01:13:45.860 --> 01:13:48.410
So in the case of
text, you would

01:13:48.410 --> 01:13:53.270
have a dictionary of tokens
that are mapped to embeddings.

01:13:53.270 --> 01:13:56.930
But here, you have something
that is more continuous.

01:13:56.930 --> 01:13:58.470
So you can have a linear layer.

01:13:58.470 --> 01:14:00.110
You can have a
convolutional layer.

01:14:00.110 --> 01:14:01.810
You can have
everything you want.

01:14:01.810 --> 01:14:04.970
So you have something
at that stage

01:14:04.970 --> 01:14:07.730
that gives a representation
of these patches.

01:14:07.730 --> 01:14:13.170
And then in a classification
setting, you have a token.

01:14:13.170 --> 01:14:17.970
So for example here, CLS
that will carry information

01:14:17.970 --> 01:14:22.810
regarding the class of your
image that you want to predict.

01:14:22.810 --> 01:14:28.050
So each of these can be
represented by embeddings.

01:14:28.050 --> 01:14:31.490
And we mentioned that we have
position information encoded

01:14:31.490 --> 01:14:32.770
in them.

01:14:32.770 --> 01:14:39.370
And next class, we're going to
see more about position encoding

01:14:39.370 --> 01:14:40.750
and all of this.

01:14:40.750 --> 01:14:43.310
So I'm not going
to expand too much.

01:14:46.160 --> 01:14:48.320
So you have a
representation for each

01:14:48.320 --> 01:14:53.680
of these patches of your input
that you then feed your VIT.

01:14:53.680 --> 01:14:58.360
And exactly, as we mentioned,
at the output of the encoder,

01:14:58.360 --> 01:15:02.120
you can locate one
specific embedding

01:15:02.120 --> 01:15:06.620
that carries the semantic
information of your input image.

01:15:06.620 --> 01:15:13.320
So here, it would be the output
corresponding to the CLS token

01:15:13.320 --> 01:15:16.520
that here, you project
to learn a classification

01:15:16.520 --> 01:15:17.740
task for example.

01:15:17.740 --> 01:15:21.280
But let's say you are
at a representation

01:15:21.280 --> 01:15:24.540
stage of your workflow.

01:15:24.540 --> 01:15:26.640
You would just
take the embedding

01:15:26.640 --> 01:15:31.040
that you see in orange at the
top as your representation

01:15:31.040 --> 01:15:31.940
of the image.

01:15:35.360 --> 01:15:36.720
OK, great.

01:15:36.720 --> 01:15:41.960
So something that I didn't
tell you is that for texts,

01:15:41.960 --> 01:15:45.870
doing that process
is cheap in data

01:15:45.870 --> 01:15:50.270
because the task that we
use to learn these models

01:15:50.270 --> 01:15:52.650
is self-supervised by nature.

01:15:52.650 --> 01:15:57.010
We anchor on next token
prediction in the world of text,

01:15:57.010 --> 01:15:59.770
and you have a bunch of
text in the internet.

01:15:59.770 --> 01:16:06.030
It's easy to have such data,
and it's easy to learn.

01:16:06.030 --> 01:16:08.230
But in the image
world, if you want

01:16:08.230 --> 01:16:11.790
to handcraft a similar task
that is as meaningful as what

01:16:11.790 --> 01:16:15.830
we have in the text world,
we have a bit more trouble.

01:16:15.830 --> 01:16:18.870
One of these candidates
that I just shown

01:16:18.870 --> 01:16:22.550
is the classification task,
for example, for which you

01:16:22.550 --> 01:16:24.830
need to gather labels.

01:16:24.830 --> 01:16:27.070
So it's quite expensive.

01:16:27.070 --> 01:16:33.150
And I just want to say
that one follow up work

01:16:33.150 --> 01:16:36.510
that works on embedding
of images that we're not

01:16:36.510 --> 01:16:42.790
going to see in details today
is Dino self-distillation

01:16:42.790 --> 01:16:46.590
with no Labels for
which the concept is

01:16:46.590 --> 01:16:52.390
to have a teacher and a
student model that receive

01:16:52.390 --> 01:16:55.070
the different kinds of inputs.

01:16:55.070 --> 01:16:59.950
And they aim at learning
similar probability distribution

01:16:59.950 --> 01:17:02.590
in the output that
are prototypes.

01:17:02.590 --> 01:17:05.830
So I think it's a
very interesting paper

01:17:05.830 --> 01:17:09.590
to see how we can
build representations

01:17:09.590 --> 01:17:12.530
out of no labels, just out
of the images themselves.

01:17:15.830 --> 01:17:17.810
So OK.

01:17:17.810 --> 01:17:18.310
Great.

01:17:18.310 --> 01:17:24.990
So now, we went through how we
can represent text and images.

01:17:24.990 --> 01:17:27.690
So did everything
make sense so far?

01:17:27.690 --> 01:17:29.590
So we went slightly
fast, but I just

01:17:29.590 --> 01:17:35.270
want to make sure we
got the gist out of it.

01:17:35.270 --> 01:17:38.530
Yeah, the comment is
in the image world.

01:17:38.530 --> 01:17:41.740
Do these images
become tokenized?

01:17:41.740 --> 01:17:44.180
So you can think
of them that way.

01:17:44.180 --> 01:17:46.100
The only difference
is that we don't

01:17:46.100 --> 01:17:49.380
have a dictionary of tokens,
but rather we look at them

01:17:49.380 --> 01:17:50.707
in a continuous manner.

01:17:50.707 --> 01:17:53.040
Because in the text world,
you would just have a lookup.

01:17:53.040 --> 01:17:54.440
But here, a learnable.

01:17:54.440 --> 01:17:57.860
So that's exactly right.

01:17:57.860 --> 01:17:58.700
Yeah.

01:17:58.700 --> 01:18:03.100
So the comment is depending
on how you cut your image,

01:18:03.100 --> 01:18:04.240
you can have errors.

01:18:04.240 --> 01:18:05.280
Yeah, totally right.

01:18:05.280 --> 01:18:07.280
And this is why it's
a hyperparameter.

01:18:07.280 --> 01:18:10.220
This ViT model comes with
different batch sizes,

01:18:10.220 --> 01:18:12.260
and this is one
of the trade offs.

01:18:12.260 --> 01:18:15.740
Yeah, so I mean, absolutely.

01:18:15.740 --> 01:18:17.260
OK, awesome.

01:18:17.260 --> 01:18:20.780
So now that we have seen how
to represent text and images

01:18:20.780 --> 01:18:23.580
separately, you might
wonder, OK, hey,

01:18:23.580 --> 01:18:26.480
I want to generate a new
image with conditioning.

01:18:26.480 --> 01:18:28.660
That depends on
both, let's say, were

01:18:28.660 --> 01:18:31.820
I want to condition
on either, but I

01:18:31.820 --> 01:18:34.820
don't want to have to
deal with two spaces.

01:18:34.820 --> 01:18:39.610
So how would you
bring them together?

01:18:39.610 --> 01:18:45.970
So what we want is to learn
a relationship between text

01:18:45.970 --> 01:18:51.690
and image that has this nice
property of when you look

01:18:51.690 --> 01:18:57.490
at the embedding space,
you want similar concepts,

01:18:57.490 --> 01:19:00.490
either image text to
be grouped together,

01:19:00.490 --> 01:19:03.290
and dissimilar ones
to be pushed away

01:19:03.290 --> 01:19:08.250
from each other with
respect to some metric.

01:19:08.250 --> 01:19:11.330
So let's take the
example of our Teddy bear

01:19:11.330 --> 01:19:13.510
and something totally unrelated.

01:19:13.510 --> 01:19:15.730
Let's say a water polo ball.

01:19:15.730 --> 01:19:23.650
So the Teddy bear with the text
token, Teddy bear is similar.

01:19:23.650 --> 01:19:26.970
Same with the water polo ball
image and the water polo ball

01:19:26.970 --> 01:19:28.770
token.

01:19:28.770 --> 01:19:32.050
But the opposite pairs are nuts.

01:19:32.050 --> 01:19:35.880
And we want a way to
quantify each situation

01:19:35.880 --> 01:19:38.160
and learn it automatically.

01:19:38.160 --> 01:19:41.000
So how can we do that?

01:19:41.000 --> 01:19:44.180
So let's build a
matrix of these two.

01:19:44.180 --> 01:19:46.880
So this is just phrasing
in a different way What.

01:19:46.880 --> 01:19:48.840
I've just said.

01:19:48.840 --> 01:19:52.280
And what we want is Teddy
bear and the image of Teddy

01:19:52.280 --> 01:19:55.120
bear to be of high similarity.

01:19:55.120 --> 01:20:01.040
So what we're going to do
is project these embeddings

01:20:01.040 --> 01:20:04.480
that we talked about
in the same space.

01:20:04.480 --> 01:20:09.000
So we're going to build these
vectors with the representations

01:20:09.000 --> 01:20:11.520
that we just saw before.

01:20:11.520 --> 01:20:13.860
So what we're doing
here is the same.

01:20:13.860 --> 01:20:18.240
So we have our patches of
images alongside the CLS token

01:20:18.240 --> 01:20:21.920
that you pass through
the VIT architecture.

01:20:21.920 --> 01:20:28.120
And then once you get the
last embedding representation,

01:20:28.120 --> 01:20:31.880
what we're going to do is
to project it in a space

01:20:31.880 --> 01:20:35.350
that we're going to have it
shared with the tech space.

01:20:35.350 --> 01:20:39.150
So the property of that
projection layer that we built

01:20:39.150 --> 01:20:43.750
is that it's going to output
a vector of same dimension

01:20:43.750 --> 01:20:47.110
by construction as
the one for text.

01:20:47.110 --> 01:20:49.150
So we do that here.

01:20:49.150 --> 01:20:54.390
And then we do the same
for the text world.

01:20:54.390 --> 01:20:56.590
So by the way, I
mentioned to you

01:20:56.590 --> 01:21:00.450
that we would look at embeddings
at the end of the encoder layer.

01:21:00.450 --> 01:21:04.190
And here, I'm showing
another procedure

01:21:04.190 --> 01:21:10.150
to get embeddings of text, which
is to pass all of these tokens

01:21:10.150 --> 01:21:14.830
to the decoder and decode
the one corresponding

01:21:14.830 --> 01:21:16.630
to the last token.

01:21:16.630 --> 01:21:19.390
And by construction of
the attention mechanism

01:21:19.390 --> 01:21:22.070
and the transformer,
these embedding

01:21:22.070 --> 01:21:24.590
coming out of the
last token is going

01:21:24.590 --> 01:21:26.770
to represent the whole sentence.

01:21:26.770 --> 01:21:29.590
So it's another
equivalent way of showing

01:21:29.590 --> 01:21:31.950
the representation of tokens.

01:21:31.950 --> 01:21:36.060
And we're going to take that
representation and project it,

01:21:36.060 --> 01:21:39.820
as we said, in that
same shared space.

01:21:39.820 --> 01:21:46.460
And by construction, these
u and v are now comparable.

01:21:46.460 --> 01:21:47.840
You can do dot products.

01:21:47.840 --> 01:21:49.980
You can compute similarities.

01:21:49.980 --> 01:21:55.420
And we define these
similarity S variable

01:21:55.420 --> 01:21:59.980
that is the scaled dot product.

01:21:59.980 --> 01:22:04.980
And one thing that
we can do now is

01:22:04.980 --> 01:22:08.820
to transform this
notion of similarity

01:22:08.820 --> 01:22:13.580
into some score that can be seen
as a probability distribution

01:22:13.580 --> 01:22:16.860
through the softmax operator.

01:22:16.860 --> 01:22:19.860
So if you compute the
exponential of the similarity

01:22:19.860 --> 01:22:22.220
between image Teddy
bear and Teddy

01:22:22.220 --> 01:22:26.460
bear over the sum of the
exponentials of this image Teddy

01:22:26.460 --> 01:22:29.940
bear and all the
other text tokens,

01:22:29.940 --> 01:22:32.290
you find the
probability distribution

01:22:32.290 --> 01:22:37.970
that this image corresponds
to that piece of text.

01:22:37.970 --> 01:22:43.210
So you can do that from a fixed
image to the set of texts.

01:22:43.210 --> 01:22:46.870
And you can do that
on the other hand,

01:22:46.870 --> 01:22:52.610
with a fixed piece of text
token to all the images.

01:22:52.610 --> 01:22:54.570
So you can have both ways.

01:22:54.570 --> 01:23:00.650
And what we do is that we
can define two losses that

01:23:00.650 --> 01:23:06.970
look symmetric, that represents
respectively, how similar,

01:23:06.970 --> 01:23:11.810
like the penalization of any
error of a fixed image based

01:23:11.810 --> 01:23:15.650
on all pieces of
text and similarity

01:23:15.650 --> 01:23:19.530
error of a piece of text with
respect to all the images.

01:23:19.530 --> 01:23:22.490
So it's symmetric cross entropy.

01:23:22.490 --> 01:23:25.450
And then when we sum
these two losses,

01:23:25.450 --> 01:23:29.160
we can obtain a final
formulation that

01:23:29.160 --> 01:23:31.600
represents what we care about.

01:23:34.920 --> 01:23:40.120
So that is the key
of the CLIP model

01:23:40.120 --> 01:23:46.640
that trains the representations
of both image and text worlds

01:23:46.640 --> 01:23:51.400
into, like with this
way of training things.

01:23:51.400 --> 01:23:56.560
So what we do is that we
scrape a bunch of images

01:23:56.560 --> 01:23:58.640
and captions off
of the internet.

01:23:58.640 --> 01:24:01.240
And then for a
given batch, we say

01:24:01.240 --> 01:24:04.760
that a given image
has its associated

01:24:04.760 --> 01:24:08.200
caption as the true
label, and then

01:24:08.200 --> 01:24:11.940
all the other captions in
that batch are negatives.

01:24:11.940 --> 01:24:14.280
So it's called in
batch negatives.

01:24:14.280 --> 01:24:18.440
And you do the same thing
of captions with respect

01:24:18.440 --> 01:24:21.520
to all the images.

01:24:21.520 --> 01:24:27.550
And you obtain that way final
embeddings in the text and image

01:24:27.550 --> 01:24:31.390
encoders here that will generate
the representation that you're

01:24:31.390 --> 01:24:33.870
looking for.

01:24:33.870 --> 01:24:38.850
So here, just to give
some order of magnitude,

01:24:38.850 --> 01:24:45.470
the paper talked about
400M pairs of data.

01:24:45.470 --> 01:24:48.710
And it's great
reach great accuracy

01:24:48.710 --> 01:24:51.510
even unsupervised tasks.

01:24:51.510 --> 01:24:55.870
But the issue here
is this softmax.

01:24:55.870 --> 01:24:59.130
So every time you have
a huge batch of data,

01:24:59.130 --> 01:25:04.950
you need to build a similarity
matrix of a fixed image

01:25:04.950 --> 01:25:07.850
with respect to all the
captions and symmetrically.

01:25:07.850 --> 01:25:12.350
And this is not a
great computationally.

01:25:12.350 --> 01:25:15.590
And also, from a
mining standpoint,

01:25:15.590 --> 01:25:18.190
what you're truly
saying in your loss is

01:25:18.190 --> 01:25:22.790
that you want to maximize the
similarity of your true image

01:25:22.790 --> 01:25:27.860
caption pair, but you don't
it's truly enforce the fact

01:25:27.860 --> 01:25:32.660
that all the other similarities
should be each very small.

01:25:32.660 --> 01:25:39.820
So some follow up work reframes
this problem into a sigmoid loss

01:25:39.820 --> 01:25:40.320
one.

01:25:40.320 --> 01:25:47.500
So instead of saying I want this
one image to have a probability

01:25:47.500 --> 01:25:51.740
distribution that matches
that of the true caption,

01:25:51.740 --> 01:25:55.660
we take one caption and
one image separately,

01:25:55.660 --> 01:26:00.020
and we ask ourselves the
question, do they match or not?

01:26:00.020 --> 01:26:04.180
And by doing so you don't have
all this similarity matrix

01:26:04.180 --> 01:26:05.540
complexities.

01:26:05.540 --> 01:26:10.700
And also, you have a stronger
statement at your loss level

01:26:10.700 --> 01:26:14.980
that's pushes the positives
together and the negatives

01:26:14.980 --> 01:26:17.300
further apart.

01:26:17.300 --> 01:26:25.210
OK, so any questions on this
contrastive learning part?

01:26:25.210 --> 01:26:25.890
Yep?

01:26:25.890 --> 01:26:28.690
The question is, do you start
from a pre-trained encoder,

01:26:28.690 --> 01:26:30.470
and what weights do you change?

01:26:30.470 --> 01:26:32.930
So I think in the paper,
they start from scratch.

01:26:32.930 --> 01:26:35.530
And they train both
encoders from scratch.

01:26:35.530 --> 01:26:38.850
You could very well
freeze or start

01:26:38.850 --> 01:26:40.970
with some pre-trained encoder.

01:26:40.970 --> 01:26:42.550
Freeze all the layers.

01:26:42.550 --> 01:26:43.550
Let the last layer.

01:26:43.550 --> 01:26:46.690
I think typically, you
would just train everything.

01:26:46.690 --> 01:26:51.590
And in real life, I think you
just wouldn't train anything.

01:26:51.590 --> 01:26:54.410
You would just pick something
out of Hugging Face.

01:26:54.410 --> 01:26:57.730
But yeah, in
practice, since it's

01:26:57.730 --> 01:27:00.090
easy to get all this
data-- so typically,

01:27:00.090 --> 01:27:02.050
you would scrape the internet.

01:27:02.050 --> 01:27:06.310
Look at images, and look
at all tags in HTML.

01:27:06.310 --> 01:27:07.630
So you can get all these pairs.

01:27:07.630 --> 01:27:10.730
So I think you're
in such a setup,

01:27:10.730 --> 01:27:13.150
where doing it from
scratch makes sense,

01:27:13.150 --> 01:27:15.370
but what you suggested
makes sense as well.

01:27:15.370 --> 01:27:16.130
Yeah.

01:27:16.130 --> 01:27:20.010
So I think it depends
on what works best.

01:27:20.010 --> 01:27:20.730
Yeah.

01:27:20.730 --> 01:27:23.200
So the question is the
transformer architecture

01:27:23.200 --> 01:27:25.700
a prerequisite for this task.

01:27:25.700 --> 01:27:26.380
Not at all.

01:27:26.380 --> 01:27:28.560
So you can do it
with any encoders.

01:27:28.560 --> 01:27:30.720
And the reason why I
talked about transformers

01:27:30.720 --> 01:27:34.920
is to ground the way of encoding
into what people do in practice.

01:27:34.920 --> 01:27:38.560
But you don't have to have this
architecture as a prerequisite.

01:27:38.560 --> 01:27:41.200
Yeah.

01:27:41.200 --> 01:27:42.660
OK, awesome.

01:27:42.660 --> 01:27:45.360
So now, we're going to
reach the most exciting part

01:27:45.360 --> 01:27:50.800
of the class, which is finally,
how do you guide our generation

01:27:50.800 --> 01:27:53.400
model with the condition.

01:27:53.400 --> 01:27:59.760
And we're going to go back to
thinking about what we discussed

01:27:59.760 --> 01:28:01.600
these past lectures.

01:28:01.600 --> 01:28:04.360
So we had this
diffusion mechanism,

01:28:04.360 --> 01:28:08.360
where you are at a given
step here, xt plus 1.

01:28:08.360 --> 01:28:14.400
And you want to find the less
noisy version of the image xt.

01:28:14.400 --> 01:28:17.480
And the thing that
we want to do now

01:28:17.480 --> 01:28:21.730
is to do that with a
condition that I called y,

01:28:21.730 --> 01:28:25.350
but that you might see
called c in papers.

01:28:25.350 --> 01:28:28.750
So you want to guide the
generation towards generating

01:28:28.750 --> 01:28:31.450
a sample that follows y.

01:28:34.390 --> 01:28:37.630
So a first idea that
you might have is OK,

01:28:37.630 --> 01:28:39.510
it's a classification.

01:28:39.510 --> 01:28:41.650
There is a concept
of classification.

01:28:41.650 --> 01:28:46.470
I can come up with a
classifier of weights phi

01:28:46.470 --> 01:28:48.230
and try to make it work.

01:28:48.230 --> 01:28:50.350
So how could you make it work?

01:28:50.350 --> 01:28:53.270
You start from this
probability distribution

01:28:53.270 --> 01:28:55.430
that you want to sample from.

01:28:55.430 --> 01:28:59.270
And then by applying
Bayes' rule,

01:28:59.270 --> 01:29:03.310
like the conditional version
with the right arguments

01:29:03.310 --> 01:29:09.110
for A, B, and C, you obtain
this product of two probability

01:29:09.110 --> 01:29:12.470
distributions that you
can see on the left

01:29:12.470 --> 01:29:18.340
represents the one step
generation process that we know.

01:29:18.340 --> 01:29:22.580
And on the right side, you have
the probability distribution

01:29:22.580 --> 01:29:26.100
of y given xt, which
would typically be what

01:29:26.100 --> 01:29:29.500
the classifier would give you.

01:29:29.500 --> 01:29:33.380
So now, let's see how we
could sample from this.

01:29:33.380 --> 01:29:36.700
So taking a look at the
probability distribution

01:29:36.700 --> 01:29:37.520
on the left.

01:29:37.520 --> 01:29:39.940
So if we recall
the first lecture,

01:29:39.940 --> 01:29:45.060
let's say in the DDPM world,
you know that this probability

01:29:45.060 --> 01:29:48.860
distribution follows a
normal distribution of a mean

01:29:48.860 --> 01:29:52.820
that we learn and
of variance that

01:29:52.820 --> 01:29:54.320
is following a noise schedule.

01:29:54.320 --> 01:29:56.280
So we don't learn it.

01:29:56.280 --> 01:29:59.180
It's something that we have.

01:29:59.180 --> 01:30:01.580
So we know the
expression of this.

01:30:01.580 --> 01:30:07.060
If you look at the probability
distribution of this,

01:30:07.060 --> 01:30:11.380
it's something that we know.

01:30:11.380 --> 01:30:13.340
So we have that on one hand.

01:30:13.340 --> 01:30:19.370
And on the other, we look at
our probability distribution

01:30:19.370 --> 01:30:21.930
at the end of the classifier.

01:30:21.930 --> 01:30:27.710
So here, why are we
not happy with that--

01:30:27.710 --> 01:30:30.830
I mean, we have the data of
the probability distribution.

01:30:30.830 --> 01:30:34.290
You just have to put the noisy
image to your classifier,

01:30:34.290 --> 01:30:35.510
and then you have it.

01:30:35.510 --> 01:30:37.110
You have a probability
distribution.

01:30:37.110 --> 01:30:39.210
Why are we not happy with that?

01:30:39.210 --> 01:30:44.810
It's because we cannot plug it
into that product equation that

01:30:44.810 --> 01:30:47.950
we saw and then
easily sample from it.

01:30:47.950 --> 01:30:50.810
We want to reframe
it in a way that we

01:30:50.810 --> 01:30:55.410
can have this nice Gaussian
distribution that we

01:30:55.410 --> 01:30:59.650
had, for example, at the
unconditioned generation step.

01:30:59.650 --> 01:31:03.570
So what we do is that
we have a trick that

01:31:03.570 --> 01:31:07.690
expands this probability
distribution with a Taylor

01:31:07.690 --> 01:31:08.970
expansion.

01:31:08.970 --> 01:31:13.090
And then you come
up with a first term

01:31:13.090 --> 01:31:16.960
that is a function of xt.

01:31:16.960 --> 01:31:22.780
And when you plug
the probability,

01:31:22.780 --> 01:31:27.400
I mean the log of p phi that we
just found into this product,

01:31:27.400 --> 01:31:29.720
you see that the
probability distribution

01:31:29.720 --> 01:31:33.780
that you want to learn is simply
the same one that you had,

01:31:33.780 --> 01:31:35.100
but with a shifted mean.

01:31:39.160 --> 01:31:41.260
So I just want to take
a pause for a second.

01:31:41.260 --> 01:31:45.440
So we went through
a process that where

01:31:45.440 --> 01:31:47.680
we tried to find
an easy probability

01:31:47.680 --> 01:31:49.280
distribution to sample from.

01:31:49.280 --> 01:31:51.180
So we went through a
series of equations.

01:31:51.180 --> 01:31:54.320
But all I want you to
know from all of that

01:31:54.320 --> 01:31:59.440
is that we tried to get
to this state, where

01:31:59.440 --> 01:32:02.260
we can sample from
an easy distribution,

01:32:02.260 --> 01:32:03.460
and that's what we reached.

01:32:03.460 --> 01:32:05.560
So in some way, we're happy.

01:32:05.560 --> 01:32:07.800
And then here,
what's good about it

01:32:07.800 --> 01:32:10.040
is that you don't
need to retrain

01:32:10.040 --> 01:32:11.620
your unconditioned model.

01:32:11.620 --> 01:32:15.430
So your theta and your
sigma t are things that.

01:32:15.430 --> 01:32:19.970
And there is a downside to it.

01:32:19.970 --> 01:32:21.850
Now, I mentioned classifiers.

01:32:21.850 --> 01:32:26.990
You need to learn a classifier
that operates on noisy images.

01:32:26.990 --> 01:32:30.830
And the process here
is that you need

01:32:30.830 --> 01:32:34.350
to have that classifier that
predicts some probability

01:32:34.350 --> 01:32:38.910
distribution, and then you
need to take the backward pass

01:32:38.910 --> 01:32:42.030
on the network in order
to find the gradient.

01:32:42.030 --> 01:32:43.370
Did that make sense so far?

01:32:49.030 --> 01:32:50.130
OK, great.

01:32:50.130 --> 01:32:52.710
So when we sample
from the distribution

01:32:52.710 --> 01:32:56.510
that I mentioned in practice,
one thing that people see

01:32:56.510 --> 01:32:59.310
is that it doesn't
produce samples that

01:32:59.310 --> 01:33:02.710
follow the y condition enough.

01:33:02.710 --> 01:33:08.030
So this is why you see that
W factor being introduced

01:33:08.030 --> 01:33:11.500
that can be tuned to
force the generation

01:33:11.500 --> 01:33:16.540
process to follow the
classifier's gradients more.

01:33:16.540 --> 01:33:21.180
And this is what we
call the guidance part.

01:33:21.180 --> 01:33:23.780
So we saw that we
don't have to retrain

01:33:23.780 --> 01:33:26.620
the unconditioned model
in order to sample

01:33:26.620 --> 01:33:28.360
from this distribution.

01:33:28.360 --> 01:33:31.660
But on the other hand, you
need to have a classifier that

01:33:31.660 --> 01:33:37.100
operates on top of a domain that
can be composed of noisy images.

01:33:37.100 --> 01:33:39.500
And the bad thing about
it is that you typically

01:33:39.500 --> 01:33:41.300
don't have that out of the box.

01:33:41.300 --> 01:33:43.540
So it's typically
something that you

01:33:43.540 --> 01:33:47.620
have to train as part of
your training process,

01:33:47.620 --> 01:33:49.820
which is a downside.

01:33:49.820 --> 01:33:55.500
And also, the fact that you need
to compute the backwards pass

01:33:55.500 --> 01:33:58.660
of your classifier
is quite expensive,

01:33:58.660 --> 01:34:01.260
which is also not great.

01:34:01.260 --> 01:34:04.260
And something else
that I will mention

01:34:04.260 --> 01:34:08.780
is that you not only need to
have that extra classifier

01:34:08.780 --> 01:34:12.730
and spend all these budgets
to compute the gradients,

01:34:12.730 --> 01:34:20.330
but you need to make sure that
the signal of your gradients

01:34:20.330 --> 01:34:26.670
scales well with the coefficient
w that you introduce.

01:34:26.670 --> 01:34:28.650
So you need some
hyperparameter tuning

01:34:28.650 --> 01:34:35.690
to make sure that the operation
that you're doing is meaningful.

01:34:35.690 --> 01:34:39.170
So one question that
you might ask yourself,

01:34:39.170 --> 01:34:43.690
is it possible to do this guided
generation without the needs

01:34:43.690 --> 01:34:45.610
of a classifier?

01:34:45.610 --> 01:34:49.010
And the answer is yes.

01:34:49.010 --> 01:34:52.770
So if you use
Bayes' formula, you

01:34:52.770 --> 01:34:57.650
can see that you can define
an implicit classifier,

01:34:57.650 --> 01:35:06.810
so called p of y given x
based on the generation,

01:35:06.810 --> 01:35:10.920
like the unconditioned
generation of your image

01:35:10.920 --> 01:35:13.720
and the conditioned
generation of your image,

01:35:13.720 --> 01:35:16.120
so conditioned on y.

01:35:16.120 --> 01:35:19.320
Which means that if
you have a network that

01:35:19.320 --> 01:35:23.320
can generate an image
without any conditions

01:35:23.320 --> 01:35:27.080
and that can generate an
image with a given condition,

01:35:27.080 --> 01:35:31.360
then you can solve this problem
by plugging this equation

01:35:31.360 --> 01:35:35.640
back into what we had,
which is what we do here.

01:35:35.640 --> 01:35:40.680
So the equation that
I showed regarding

01:35:40.680 --> 01:35:46.080
the distribution of
the next sampling step

01:35:46.080 --> 01:35:49.200
can be written in epsilon space.

01:35:49.200 --> 01:35:53.380
So I mentioned this Gaussian
distribution of shifted mean.

01:35:53.380 --> 01:35:55.600
You can rewrite
the noise that you

01:35:55.600 --> 01:36:00.600
want to predict into one
that takes into account

01:36:00.600 --> 01:36:03.520
that classifier signal.

01:36:03.520 --> 01:36:06.510
And what the
authors of the paper

01:36:06.510 --> 01:36:09.670
of the classifier free
guidance paper did

01:36:09.670 --> 01:36:14.270
is that they plugged
in the P phi.

01:36:14.270 --> 01:36:18.230
Expression with the
implicit classifier,

01:36:18.230 --> 01:36:21.990
the expression that I
just mentioned before.

01:36:21.990 --> 01:36:25.110
And they obtained this
very nice expression

01:36:25.110 --> 01:36:28.110
that is a function
of the noise that we

01:36:28.110 --> 01:36:34.070
want to remove based on a model
that isn't fed any condition,

01:36:34.070 --> 01:36:38.950
and one for which a
condition is provided.

01:36:38.950 --> 01:36:41.070
And what you can
see is that there

01:36:41.070 --> 01:36:44.830
is this w term in
the middle that

01:36:44.830 --> 01:36:46.750
has about the same
interpretation as the one

01:36:46.750 --> 01:36:53.030
that we saw before that
will tweak the generation

01:36:53.030 --> 01:36:58.310
to be more or less towards
the condition that you bring.

01:36:58.310 --> 01:36:59.370
So there's that.

01:36:59.370 --> 01:37:02.390
And then now, we are
happy because we no longer

01:37:02.390 --> 01:37:05.520
have to train separate
classifier phi.

01:37:05.520 --> 01:37:11.720
We can just rely on the
generation network's weights.

01:37:11.720 --> 01:37:15.980
So how does that look
like in practice?

01:37:15.980 --> 01:37:18.820
So with a certain
probability called

01:37:18.820 --> 01:37:21.860
the unconditional
probability, what

01:37:21.860 --> 01:37:26.660
you can do is sample an image
from your training set, and time

01:37:26.660 --> 01:37:30.440
step, and noise, and try to
predict the noise from it,

01:37:30.440 --> 01:37:33.620
which is what we
saw in the past.

01:37:33.620 --> 01:37:40.300
And with probability 1 minus p
unconditional, what you can do

01:37:40.300 --> 01:37:42.700
is do the same.

01:37:42.700 --> 01:37:46.260
But then use the embeddings
that we saw in the parts

01:37:46.260 --> 01:37:51.820
just before to feed that network
with this additional information

01:37:51.820 --> 01:37:54.260
and predict the
quantity of noise

01:37:54.260 --> 01:37:58.340
to remove with the condition y.

01:37:58.340 --> 01:38:05.370
So you do that 1 minus
p and conditional times.

01:38:05.370 --> 01:38:10.850
And what you get is
something interesting,

01:38:10.850 --> 01:38:17.950
which is if you look at how
your predictions look like,

01:38:17.950 --> 01:38:20.650
so let's say the noise
that you want to remove

01:38:20.650 --> 01:38:23.810
is the points top left,
and then the noise that you

01:38:23.810 --> 01:38:25.530
want to remove when
you have a condition

01:38:25.530 --> 01:38:27.250
is the one in the middle.

01:38:27.250 --> 01:38:31.130
Then the guidance
hyperparameter w

01:38:31.130 --> 01:38:35.530
will push the
prediction of the noise

01:38:35.530 --> 01:38:40.370
more towards the noise
of the condition sample.

01:38:40.370 --> 01:38:43.010
And the reason why we
do that is that because

01:38:43.010 --> 01:38:49.110
in practice, if you don't have
this hyperparameter, you tend.

01:38:49.110 --> 01:38:53.130
And if you just use the
condition sample noise

01:38:53.130 --> 01:38:57.530
as the one that you use to
denoise your noisy latents,

01:38:57.530 --> 01:39:03.400
you might obtain images that
do not follow your prompts very

01:39:03.400 --> 01:39:04.005
closely.

01:39:04.005 --> 01:39:05.880
And this is why we have
such a hyperparameter

01:39:05.880 --> 01:39:08.920
in the first place.

01:39:08.920 --> 01:39:13.440
In terms of numbers, the
guidance parameter is typically

01:39:13.440 --> 01:39:18.200
set to just a few,
I think three.

01:39:18.200 --> 01:39:21.560
In the paper, so
typically more than one.

01:39:21.560 --> 01:39:24.080
And I just want to
put a disclaimer here

01:39:24.080 --> 01:39:29.920
that the equations that I showed
with the classifier guidance

01:39:29.920 --> 01:39:33.100
hyperparameter might
have several definitions.

01:39:33.100 --> 01:39:38.840
So you might see formulas
with w in there, w plus 1.

01:39:38.840 --> 01:39:41.720
So all these values
of w that you take

01:39:41.720 --> 01:39:46.120
are dependent on the equation
that you operate from.

01:39:46.120 --> 01:39:47.200
So this is 1.

01:39:47.200 --> 01:39:49.360
And then the other thing
is that in practice

01:39:49.360 --> 01:39:53.960
at training time, the
proportion of time for which you

01:39:53.960 --> 01:40:01.070
show unconditioned generation
is typically 10%, 20% of time.

01:40:01.070 --> 01:40:05.790
And this time, you
do not need to have

01:40:05.790 --> 01:40:08.430
an additional classifier
at hand and to do

01:40:08.430 --> 01:40:10.910
an expensive backward pass.

01:40:10.910 --> 01:40:15.330
But you still need to
do two inference steps.

01:40:15.330 --> 01:40:19.230
Because we saw that the
equation has this epsilon

01:40:19.230 --> 01:40:23.790
theta of xt and no condition,
and the one with the condition

01:40:23.790 --> 01:40:25.590
which is 2 forward passes.

01:40:25.590 --> 01:40:27.410
So you still have
some extra cost,

01:40:27.410 --> 01:40:31.070
although it's better
than the other technique.

01:40:31.070 --> 01:40:38.230
And this technique, CFG is the
one that's most used today.

01:40:38.230 --> 01:40:39.730
OK.

01:40:39.730 --> 01:40:40.970
So any questions?

01:40:47.070 --> 01:40:48.230
OK, great.

01:40:48.230 --> 01:40:50.810
With that, I hope you
have a great weekend.

01:40:50.810 --> 01:40:54.460
[APPLAUSE]

